\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + \\frac{π}{3} &= \\frac{4π}{3} + 2nπ, \\mid 2x + \\frac{π}{3}|= \\frac{5π}{3} + 2nπ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}2x &= π + 2nπ & 2x|= \\frac{4π}{3} + 2nπ \\\\ x|= \\frac{π}{2} + nπ & x|= \\frac{2π}{3} + nπ \\\\ x|= \\frac{π}{2} + \\frac{2nπ}{2} & x|= \\frac{2π}{3} + \\frac{3nπ}{3} \\\\ x|= ((1 + 2n)π)/(2) & x|= (π(2 + 3n))/(3)\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set\n\n$$\nn=0, \\pm 1, \\pm 2,\\ldots\n$$\n\nuntil we have generated all solutions in the domain\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\lt 2\\pi\n$$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$ and $x=\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3}$.\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$ and $x=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}$.\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\lt 2\\pi\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions $x_1,x_2,x_3,x_4$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{2\\pi}{3},\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},\\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nx_4 = \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\nx_1\\cdot x_4 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\cdot \\dfrac{5\\pi}{3} = \\dfrac{5\\pi^2}{6}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 false] [17592186091992 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nFirst, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})-3 & =0 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\frac{3}{-2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & 2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n