} + x_{3}$?\n\n> A calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535918 17592186045417 false] [17592186092836 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nFirst, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-\\frac{7\\pi }{3})+1 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-\\frac{7\\pi }{3}) & =-1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-\\frac{7\\pi }{3}) & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-\\frac{7\\pi }{3}) & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{7\\pi }{3} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & 2x-\\frac{7\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =\\frac{25\\pi }{12}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 2x & =\\frac{43\\pi }{12}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{25\\pi }{24}+n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{43\\pi }{24}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{25\\pi }{24}+\\frac{24n\\pi }{24} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{43\\pi }{24}+\\frac{24n\\pi }{24} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi (25+24n)}{24} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{\\pi (43+24n)}{24}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{25\\pi }{24}$ and $x=\\frac{43\\pi }{24}$\n\n- Setting $n=-1$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{24}$ and $x=\\frac{19\\pi }{24}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{24},\\frac{19\\pi }{24},\\frac{25\\pi }{24},\\frac{43\\pi }{24}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{24}$ and ${x}_{3}=\\frac{25\\pi }{24},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{24}+\\frac{25\\pi }{24}=\\frac{26\\pi }{24}=\\frac{13\\pi }{12}.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186092883 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186092883 1071 "Given that $x_{1}$ and $x_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $5\\sin x - 2\\cos x = 0$ for $0 \\le x < 2π$, what is the value of $x_{1} \\cdot x_{2}$, rounded to $1$ decimal place?\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139535918 17592186045417 false] [17592186092883 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to $1$ decimal place?" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186092883 1074 "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{2\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 5\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-2 & =0 \\\\ 5\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-2 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =2 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{2}{5})=0.380\\,506…≈0.380\\,5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places. \n\nSince $0.380\\,5$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=0.380\\,5.$\n\nTo find the other solution in the given interval, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=0.380\\,506…+\\pi =3.522\\,099…≈3.522\\,1\n$$\n\nrounded to $4$ decimal places. \n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈0.380\\,5\\cdot 3.522\\,1≈1.3\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place." 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186093390 1064 "Suppose we want to find all of the solutions to\n\n![](images/4a/4ad61501c6c9b94fa05be806aead4f481a1e1e2f1f34b8cfb346ce685424b1f9.svg)\n\nNotice that this equation contains a transformed sine function. \n\nRecall that the general solution of the elementary equation $\\sin{X} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & X & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi ,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is assumed to be any integer.\n\nTo find the general solution of the transformed sine equation, we replace $X$ with the sine function's input, which is $x-\\dfrac{\\pi}3$ in this case. This gives the general solution\n\n$$\nx - \\dfrac{\\pi}3 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + 2n\\pi, \\qquad\\qquad x - \\dfrac{\\pi}3 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} + 2n\\pi.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above two equations for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\pi +2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\frac{6n\\pi }{3} & \\, & \\, & x & =(1+2n)\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\pi (1+3n)}{3} & \\, & \\, &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set $n=0,\\pm1,\\pm2,\\ldots$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\leq x <2\\pi.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\dfrac{2\\pi}3$ and $x=\\pi.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\leq x < 2\\pi.$ So, the solutions $x_1$ and $x_2$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3},\\qquad x_2 = \\pi.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186093390 1064 "Suppose we want to find all of the solutions to\n\n$$\n0 \\le x < 2π\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation contains a transformed sine function.\n\nRecall that the general solution of the elementary equation\n\n$$\n\\sin{X} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt3}{2}\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nX &= \\frac{π}{3} + 2nπ, \\mid X|= \\frac{2π}{3} + 2nπ\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is assumed to be any integer.\n\nTo find the general solution of the transformed sine equation, we replace $X$ with the sine function's input, which is\n\n$$\nx-\\dfrac{\\pi}3\n$$\n\nin this case. This gives the general solution\n\n$$\nx - \\dfrac{\\pi}3 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3} + 2n\\pi, \\qquad\\qquad x - \\dfrac{\\pi}3 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3} + 2n\\pi\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above two equations for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= \\frac{2π}{3} + 2nπ\\begin{vmatrix}x|= π + 2nπ \\\\ x|= \\frac{2π}{3} + \\frac{6nπ}{3}\\end{vmatrix}x|= (1 + 2n)π \\\\\nx &= (2π(1 + 3n))/(3)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we set\n\n$$\nn=0,\\pm1,\\pm2,\\ldots\n$$\n\nuntil we have generated all solutions in the domain\n\n$$\n0\\leq x <2\\pi\n$$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\dfrac{2\\pi}3$ and $x=\\pi$.\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain\n\n$$\n0\\leq x < 2\\pi\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions $x_1$ and $x_2$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx_1 = \\dfrac{2\\pi}{3},\\qquad x_2 = \\pi\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 false] [17592186093446 1064 "Suppose we want to find all of the solutions to the equation\n\n![](images/03/039f2eaa5fc29449de1795a64aeb80391087cbbad093f0b1b8e3533bb024687f.svg)\n\nTo calculate a solution, we can reduce the equation to a more convenient form by dividing through by $\\cos x,$ and then use the identity $\\tan x = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}$ to simplify. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x-\\sqrt{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\tan x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nWe can immediately calculate the principal value $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx = \\arctan(\\sqrt{3}) = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is $x_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$\n\nTo find the other solution in the given interval, we add $\\pi\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the solutions are $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}, \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186093446 1064 "Suppose we want to find all of the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\n0\\le x<2\\pi .\n$$\n\nTo calculate a solution, we can reduce the equation to a more convenient form by dividing through by $\\cos x,$ and then use the identity $\\tan x = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}$ to simplify. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x}-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}x}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}x}{\\operatorname{cos}x}-\\sqrt{3} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}x-\\sqrt{3} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\tan x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\sqrt{3}\n$$\n\nWe can immediately calculate the principal value $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nx = \\arctan(\\sqrt{3}) = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}\n$$\n\nSince $\\dfrac{\\pi}{3}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is $x_1 = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}.$\n\nTo find the other solution in the given interval, we add $\\pi\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the solutions are $x = \\dfrac{\\pi}{3}, \\dfrac{4\\pi}{3}.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 false] [17592186093447 1064 "L