953 1071 "What is the inverse of $f(x) = -x^2 + 2x + 6$ for $x\\geq 1$?" 13194139535918 17592186045417 false] [17592186183953 1071 "What is the inverse of $f(x) = -x^2 + 2x + 6$ for $x\\geq 1?$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186183953 1074 "First, we complete the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x) &= -x^{2} + 2x + 6 \\\\\n&= - (x^{2} - 2x) + 6 \\\\\n&= - (x^{2} - 2 \\cdot x + 1^{2} - 1^{2}) + 6 \\\\\n&= - ((x - 1)^{2} - 1) + 6 \\\\\n&= - (x - 1)^{2} + 1 + 6 \\\\\n&= - (x - 1)^{2} + 7\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$.\n\n$$\ny = -(x-1)^2+ 7\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$.\n\n$$\nx = -(y-1)^2+ 7\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= - (y - 1)^{2} + 7 \\\\\nx - 7 &= - (y - 1)^{2} \\\\\n7 - x &= (y - 1)^{2} \\\\\n\\sqrt{7 - x} &= y - 1 \\\\\n\\sqrt{7 - x} + 1 &= y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we selected the positive square root because if the domain of $f(x)$ is\n\n$$\nx \\geq 1\n$$\n\nthen the range of $f^{-1}(x)$ is\n\n$$\nf^{-1} \\geq 1\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with $f^{-1}(x)$.\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\sqrt{7-x} +1\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 false] [17592186183953 1074 "First, we complete the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-{x}^{2}+2x+6 \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-2x)+6 \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-2\\cdot x+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2})+6 \\\\  & =-((x-1)^{2}-1)+6 \\\\  & =-(x-1)^{2}+1+6 \\\\  & =-(x-1)^{2}+7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y.$\n\n$$\ny =  -(x-1)^2+ 7\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y.$\n\n$$\nx = -(y-1)^2+ 7\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-(y-1)^{2}+7 \\\\ x-7 & =-(y-1)^{2} \\\\ 7-x & =(y-1)^{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{7-x} & =y-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{7-x}+1 & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we selected the positive square root because if the domain of $f(x)$ is $x \\geq 1,$ then the range of $f^{-1}(x)$ is  $f^{-1} \\geq 1.$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with $f^{-1}(x).$\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = \\sqrt{7-x } +1\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186183954 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x) = (x - 3)^2 - 2$ for $x \\leq 3$." 13194139535918 17592186045417 false] [17592186183954 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x) = (x - 3)^2 - 2$ for $x \\leq 3.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186183954 1074 "To find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = (x-3)^2 - 2\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\nx = (y - 3)^2 - 2\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx &= (y - 3)^{2} - 2 \\\\\nx + 2 &= (y - 3)^{2} \\\\\n- \\sqrt{x + 2} &= y - 3 \\\\\n3 - \\sqrt{x + 2} &= y\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the negative square root because if\n\n$$\nx \\leq 3\n$$\n\nis the domain of $f$, then the range of the inverse is\n\n$$\nf^{-1} \\leq 3\n$$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with $f^{-1}(x){:}$\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = 3 - \\sqrt{x + 2}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 false] [17592186183954 1074 "To find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y{:}$\n\n$$\ny = (x-3)^2 - 2\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y{:}$\n\n$$\nx = (y - 3)^2 - 2\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(y-3)^{2}-2 \\\\ x+2 & =(y-3)^{2} \\\\ -\\sqrt{x+2} & =y-3 \\\\ 3-\\sqrt{x+2} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the negative square root because if $x \\leq 3$ is the domain of $f,$ then the range of the inverse is $f^{-1} \\leq 3.$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with $f^{-1}(x){:}$\n\n$$\nf^{-1}(x) = 3 - \\sqrt{x + 2}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186184078 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent line to the polar curve $r = 1+2\\cos\\theta$ at $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$, given that\n\n$\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = -\\dfrac{\\cos\\theta + 2\\cos(2\\theta)}{\\sin\\theta + 2\\sin(2\\theta)}$." 13194139535918 17592186045417 false] [17592186184078 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent line to the polar curve $r = 1+2\\cos\\theta$ at $\\theta = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ given that \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = -\\dfrac{\\cos\\theta  + 2\\cos(2\\theta)}{\\sin\\theta + 2\\sin(2\\theta)}.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186184078 1074 "To find the equation of the tangent line to a polar curve at a point $P$, we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = -\\dfrac{\\cos\\theta + 2\\cos(2\\theta)}{\\sin\\theta + 2\\sin(2\\theta)}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P$.\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{θ = π/2} \\\\\n&= - (\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}) + 2\\cos (π))/(\\sin (\\frac{π}{2}) + 2\\sin (π)) \\\\\n&= - (0 + 2(-1))/(1 + 2(0)) \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P$.\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= r\\cos θ \\\\\n&= (1 + 2\\cos θ)\\cos θ \\\\\n&= (1 + 2\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}))\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= (1 + 2 \\cdot 0) \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= 0 \\\\\ny_{1} &= r\\sin θ \\\\\n&= (1 + 2\\cos θ)\\sin θ \\\\\n&= (1 + 2\\cos (\\frac{π}{2}))\\sin (\\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= (1 + 2 \\cdot 0) \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n&= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,1)$.\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent.\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= m(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - 1 &= 2(x - 0) \\\\\ny &= 2x + 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/30/30b1aedfd6187ed1f936ef1faf25fcb8b99857103c656163a3e6897e5c6c5038.png)" 13194139535918 17592186045417 false] [17592186184078 1074 "To find the equation of the tangent line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = -\\dfrac{\\cos\\theta  + 2\\cos(2\\theta)}{\\sin\\theta + 2\\sin(2\\theta)}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P.$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )} \\\\  & =-\\frac{0+2(-1)}{1+2(0)} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =(1+2\\cdot 0)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(1+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =(1+2\\cdot 0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,1).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent.\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =2(x-0) \\\\ y & =2x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/30/30b1aedfd6187ed1f936ef1faf25fcb8b99857103c656163a3e6897e5c6c5038.png)" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186184120 1071 "![](images/fc/fc3ec38b91c71be3e9638dfa0127f1baab5d11d30e6156d863b36758f6751e7d.png)\n\nFind the equation of the **normal** line to the polar curve $r = 1 - 2\\cos\\theta$ at $\\theta = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}$, given that\n$y'(\\theta) = \\cos\\theta-2\\cos(2\\theta), \\qquad x'(\\theta) = 2\\sin(2\\theta) - \\sin\\theta$." 13194139535918 17592186045417 false] [17592186184120 1071 "![](images/fc/fc3ec38b91c71be3e9638dfa0127f1baab5d11d30e6156d863b36758f6751e7d.png)\n\nFind the equation of the **normal** line to the polar curve $r = 1 - 2\\cos\\theta$ at $\\theta = \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2},$ given that\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta)  = \\cos\\theta-2\\cos(2\\theta) , \\qquad\nx'(\\theta) = 2\\sin(2\\theta) - \\sin\\theta.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186184120 1074 "To find the equation of the normal line to a polar curve at a point $P$, we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta) = \\cos\\theta-2\\cos(2\\theta), \\qquad x'(\\theta) = 2\\sin(2\\theta) - \\sin\\theta\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{dy}{dx} &= (y^{′}(θ))/(x^{′}(θ)) \\\\\n&= (\\cos θ - 2\\cos (2θ))/(2\\sin (2θ) - \\sin θ)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P$.\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm &= \\left.\\frac{dy}{dx}\\right|_{θ = 3π/2} \\\\\n&= ((\\cos (\\frac{3π}{2}) - 2\\cos (2 \\cdot \\frac{3π}{2})))/((2\\sin (2 \\cdot \\frac{3π}{2}) - \\sin (\\frac{3π}{2}))) \\\\\n&= ((\\cos (\\frac{3π}{2}) - 2\\cos (3π)))/((2\\sin (3π) - \\sin (\\frac{3π}{2}))) \\\\\n&= (0 - 2(-1))/(0 - (-1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{0 + 2}{0 + 1} \\\\\n&= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P$.\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx_{1} &= r\\cos θ \\\\\n&= (1 - 2\\cos θ)\\cos θ \\\\\n&= (1 - 2\\cos (\\frac{3π}{2}))\\cos (\\frac{3π}{2}) \\\\\n&= (1 - 2 \\cdot 0) \\cdot 0 \\\\\n&= 0 \\\\\ny_{1} &= r\\sin θ \\\\\n&= (1 - 2\\cos θ)\\sin θ \\\\\n&= (1 - 2\\cos (\\frac{3π}{2}))\\sin (\\frac{3π}{2}) \\\\\n&= (1 - 2 \\cdot 0) \\cdot (-1) \\\\\n&= 1 \\cdot (-1) \\\\\n&=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are\n\n$$\n\\left(0, -1 \\right)\n$$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the normal.\n\nWe find the equation of the normal as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny - y_{1} &= -\\frac{1}{m}(x - x_{1}) \\\\\ny - (-1) &= -\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (x - 0) \\\\\ny + 1 &= -\\frac{1}{2}x \\\\\ny &= -\\frac{1}{2}x - 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 false] [17592186184120 1074 "To find the equation of the normal line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}.$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\ny'(\\theta)  = \\cos\\theta-2\\cos(2\\theta) , \\qquad\nx'(\\theta) = 2\\sin(2\\theta) - \\sin\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P.$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2}))}{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\pi ))}{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-2(-1)}{0-(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+2}{0+1} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}))\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =(1-2\\cdot 0)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\opera