186542109 1077 "field-1" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542109 1078 #edb/ref 17592186541529 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542109 1079 #uuid "fd131918-eda5-51aa-9124-e861cbcfd218" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542110 1075 #edb/ref 17592186541825 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542110 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542110 1077 "field-1" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542110 1078 #edb/ref 17592186541825 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542110 1079 #uuid "fdd984f9-dbf2-5ec7-aa91-9f6da7aad521" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542111 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542030 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542111 1069 "q-46396" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542111 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542111 1071 "What is the inverse of $f(x)=5-9{x}^{2}$ for $x\\ge 0?$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542111 1072 #uuid "0439240f-2359-52c3-a019-0d15653653ab" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542111 1074 "To find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny=5-9{x}^{2}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx=5-9{y}^{2}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =5-9{y}^{2} \\\\ x-5 & =-9{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{5-x}{9} & ={y}^{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{5-x}{9}} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we selected the positive square root because if $x\\ge 0$ is the domain of $f,$ then the range of the inverse is ${f}^{-1}\\ge 0.$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{5-x}}{3}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542112 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542043 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542112 1069 "q-23450" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542112 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542112 1071 "An angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane (measured in the usual way) has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={20}^{∘}.$ Which of the following cannot be $\\theta ?$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542112 1072 #uuid "072474b4-ff42-59ab-ad55-f18f16ce9f1c" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542112 1074 "Let's remind ourselves of the relationship between ${\\theta }_{R}$ and $\\theta .$\n\n![](images/a2/a2d02d14edf25a59eceb842a2cc902f07a29754f0978ec56a8293e16698ec383.png)\n\nUsing ${\\theta }_{R}={20}^{∘},$ let's calculate all possible values of $\\theta .$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the first quadrant, then $\\theta ={\\theta }_{R}={20}^{∘}.$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the second quadrant, then $\\theta ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R}={180}^{∘}-{20}^{∘}={160}^{∘}.$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the third quadrant, then $\\theta ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R}={180}^{∘}+{20}^{∘}={200}^{∘}.$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the fourth quadrant, then $\\theta ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R}={360}^{∘}-{20}^{∘}={340}^{∘}.$\n\nTherefore, $\\theta $ cannot be equal to ${260}^{∘}.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542113 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542071 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542113 1069 "q-134028" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542113 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542113 1071 "![](images/2d/2ddf870155c876bac41bd437229afb971bcb6eb6cdd67351c27a0132f7d05325.png)\n\nEstimate the slope of the trend line for the scatter plot above. Round your answer to the nearest $0.5.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542113 1072 #uuid "07871eb6-b857-5072-89f4-ba9b4061b1b7" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542113 1074 "First, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot:\n\n![](images/b7/b79dacf04634d91eae5ee8dd82a6603ed93357d6300a95e6343a683429caf506.png)\n\nIt seems that the trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})≈(4,6)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})≈(8,4).$\n\nTherefore, we estimate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ≈\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-6}{8-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{4} \\\\  & =-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542114 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542008 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542114 1069 "q-146203" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542114 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542114 1071 "| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)$ | $0.3$ | $0.45$ | $0.25$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=2]$ given that the conditional probability mass function ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)$ is shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|2}=2.2.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542114 1072 #uuid "0a174da3-b391-5e95-bb6f-67313dad4a2d" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542114 1074 "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=2] & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,2)]-{\\mu }_{X|2}^{2} \\\\  & =[(1)^{2}\\cdot 0.3+(2)^{2}\\cdot 0.45+(4)^{2}\\cdot 0.25]-(2.2)^{2} \\\\  & =0.3+1.8+4-4.84 \\\\  & =1.26.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542115 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542079 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542115 1069 "q-141252" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542115 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542115 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent line to the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )$ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )}.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542115 1072 #uuid "0c69e568-3aa6-590d-a547-523c7522fc27" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542115 1074 "To find the equation of the tangent line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )}.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )}{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{[3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})]}{[3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})]} \\\\  & =\\frac{[3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})]}{[3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})]} \\\\  & =\\frac{(3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 0+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 1)}{(3\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot 0-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})}{(-\\frac{1}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\ {y}_{1} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$ \n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent:\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{2} & =-\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (x-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\ y-\\frac{1}{2} & =-\\sqrt{3}\\,x+\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ y & =-\\sqrt{3}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/10/1018201037350f6bd231a384219d03c03fc1e2165a088e158c9ba2b2bfbb1022.png)" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542116 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542024 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542116 1069 "q-26830" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542116 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542116 1071 "Given that $\\frac{{3}^{x}}{5}=3\\cdot {4}^{x},$ what is the value of $x?$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542116 1072 #uuid "1016352e-7a56-5245-a46f-5f32ded11ee4" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542116 1074 "First, notice that, by multiplying by $5$ on both sides,  the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{3}^{x}=15\\cdot {4}^{x}\n$$\n\nNow, we take ${log}_{3}$ on both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡({3}^{x})={log}_{3}⁡(15\\cdot {4}^{x})\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{3}⁡(15\\cdot {4}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use  the product law and the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{3}⁡(15\\cdot {4}^{x}) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(15)+{log}_{3}⁡({4}^{x}) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(15)+x{log}_{3}⁡4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{3}⁡(15)+x{log}_{3}⁡4 \\\\ x-x{log}_{3}⁡4 & ={log}_{3}⁡(15) \\\\ x(1-{log}_{3}⁡4) & ={log}_{3}⁡(15) \\\\ x & =\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(15)}{1-{log}_{3}⁡4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the change of base formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(15)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡15}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}≈2.464\\,974, \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡4=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}≈1.261\\,860.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $x$ as\n\n$$\nx≈\\frac{2.464\\,974}{1-1.261\\,860}≈-9.413,\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542117 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542069 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542117 1069 "q-46395" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542117 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542117 1071 "What is the inverse of $f(x)=2{x}^{2}-6$ for $x\\ge 0?$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542117 1072 #uuid "10ace3a5-6164-50ba-ba18-c598288b7099" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542117 1074 "To find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny=2{x}^{2}-6\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx=2{y}^{2}-6\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2{y}^{2}-6 \\\\ x+6 & =2{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{x+6}{2} & ={y}^{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{x+6}{2}} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we selected the positive square root because if $x\\ge 0$ is the domain of $f,$ then the range of the inverse is ${f}^{-1}\\ge 0.$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\sqrt{\\frac{x+6}{2}}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542118 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542045 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542118 1069 "q-65877" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542118 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542118 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{x}{2}=\\frac{1}{2}$ for $0\\le x<4\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542118 1072 #uuid "12cfbabe-7848-5378-9c22-fd0a0d819de6" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542118 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & \\frac{x}{2} & =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our two equations for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}+2\\cdot 2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =2\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{6}+2\\cdot 2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+4n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}+4n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{12n\\pi }{3} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{5\\pi }{3}+\\frac{12n\\pi }{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(12n+1)\\pi }{3} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{(12n+5)\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<4\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<4\\pi .$ So, the solutions are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{3}=\\frac{5{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542119 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541973 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542119 1069 "q-242915" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542119 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542119 1071 "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${2}^{6x+1}={6}^{x+7}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542119 1072 #uuid "144d3b46-ddcf-55fb-8478-125713df09d1" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542119 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{6x+1} & ={6}^{x+7} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{6x+1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({6}^{x+7})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n(6x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)=(x+7)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =(x+7)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ 6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)+7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ 6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x(6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)) & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x & =\\frac{7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542120 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542005 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542120 1069 "q-326520" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542120 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542120 1071 "A backpack costs $$64$ before tax. If a $7.75%$ sales tax is applied, what is the total price after tax?" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542120 1072 #uuid "16908ce6-e741-598e-9537-76866b0601d0" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542120 1074 "To find the total price after tax, we apply a $7.75%$ increase to the original price.\n\nIncreasing by $7.75%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100%+7.75%=107.75%=1.0775\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original price by $1.0775:$\n\n$$\n64\\times 1.0775=68.96.\n$$\n\nSo, the total price of the backpack after tax is $$68.96.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542121 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542059 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542121 1069 "q-222071" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542121 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542121 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=-10{x}^{2}+40x-27$ for $x\\le 2.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542121 1072 #uuid "1749af1b-7a2a-52a7-adf9-0527962ea5b1" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542121 1074 "First, we complete the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-10{x}^{2}+40x-27 \\\\  & =-10({x}^{2}-4x)-27 \\\\  & =-10(({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2})-{2}^{2})-27 \\\\  & =-10((x-2)^{2}-4)-27 \\\\  & =-10{(x-2)}^{2}+40-27 \\\\  & =-10{(x-2)}^{2}+13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTo find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=-10{(x-2)}^{2}+13\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx=-10{(y-2)}^{2}+13\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-10{(y-2)}^{2}+13 \\\\ 10{(y-2)}^{2} & =13-x \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{13-x}{10} \\\\ y-2 & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{13-x}{10}} \\\\ y & =2-\\sqrt{\\frac{13-x}{10}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we selected the negative square root because if the domain of $f(x)$ is $x\\le 2,$ then the range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is  ${f}^{-1}\\le 2.$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=2-\\sqrt{\\frac{13-x}{10}}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542122 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542034 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542122 1069 "q-100180" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542122 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542122 1071 "Calculate the value of ${(-\\frac{2}{9})}^{0}.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542122 1072 #uuid "1873fa45-940e-5434-9a04-82891d309ea9" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542122 1074 "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1.$ So, \n\n$$\n{(-\\frac{2}{9})}^{0}=1.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542123 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541967 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542123 1069 "q-266057" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542123 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542123 1071 "![](images/59/591d9b81e6480a20388f873a6b4aad0d6712edec54f1b150f0c530e023157d48.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a unit circle and the angle $\\theta $ measured in the usual way. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $0.86,$ calculate the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ to the nearest integer.\n\n${\\theta }_{R}≈$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542123 1072 #uuid "1ad7aa31-ffdf-5a51-ba9d-eeaea5553edb" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542123 1074 "The reference angle is the acute angle that $\\theta $ makes with the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, we create a right-triangle by drawing a vertical line from $P$ to the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/1b/1b11bdb22a07da238cee479070ace299f89f186e1500ec44d9af84a7f37d12f5.png)\n\nSo, we have the following triangle, where ${\\theta }_{R}$ is the reference angle of $\\theta .$\n\n![](images/25/256deadcd68077b1cace4ec55d897c393b2ac849af0ba29748840a617c81e989.png)\n\nWe know that the hypotenuse has a length of $1$ because it is a radius of the unit circle. \n\nNow, using the triangle above, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{0.86}{1}=0.86.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ using the inverse cosine:\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.86)≈{31}^{∘}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542124 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542052 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542124 1069 "q-31964" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542124 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542124 1071 "$9{y}^{2}-6y+1=$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542124 1072 #uuid "1c00ad8d-9908-5ab2-b9e6-65ef0d5fcd94" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542124 1074 "The \"square of a difference\" formula states that\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-2ab+{b}^{2}=(a-b)^{2}.\n$$\n\nWe will use this formula to factor the given trinomial. Our trinomial is\n\n$$\n9{y}^{2}-6y+1.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write both the quadratic term and the constant term as perfect squares:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3y)^{2}-6y+(1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor a $2$ out of the linear term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3y)^{2}-2(3y)+(1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the middle parentheses $(3y)$ as a product of $3y$ and $1:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3y)^{2}-2(3y\\cdot 1)+(1)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we factor the trinomial using the square of difference formula with $a=3y$ and $b=1.$ This gives \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3y-1)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542125 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541994 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542125 1069 "q-64603" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542125 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542125 1071 "![](images/39/39afde1187d11693e9bebeabaa9b92f37dd56502240b6d2b23df8512b9afc79c.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the angle $\\theta ={60}^{∘}$ in the coordinate plane. What is its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}?$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542125 1072 #uuid "1c2cddbd-90ae-52b2-ba7d-84da7bc2e8d1" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542125 1074 "![](images/70/70ea24a6e98e9cab516821b7fb401ebac62557ea25319d79597915565cc61eda.png)\n\nThe reference angle, denoted ${\\theta }_{R},$  is the angle that $\\theta ={60}^{∘}$ makes with the $x$-axis.\n\nAs $\\theta $ is in the first quadrant, its reference angle is given by\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}=\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}={60}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542126 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541969 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542126 1069 "q-143084" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542126 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542126 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=2{x}^{2}-4x+7$ for $x\\ge 1.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542126 1072 #uuid "2079d901-07f2-590c-b43f-09625015f43f" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542126 1074 "First, we complete the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =2{x}^{2}-4x+7 \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}-2x)+7 \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}-2\\cdot x+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2})+7 \\\\  & =2((x-1)^{2}-1)+7 \\\\  & =2(x-1)^{2}-2+7 \\\\  & =2(x-1)^{2}+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTo find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny=2(x-1)^{2}+5\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx=2(y-1)^{2}+5\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2(y-1)^{2}+5 \\\\ x-5 & =2(y-1)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{x-5}{2} & =(y-1)^{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{x-5}{2}} & =y-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{x-5}{2}}+1 & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we selected the positive square root because if $x\\ge 1$ is the domain of $f,$ then the range of the inverse is ${f}^{-1}\\ge 1.$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\sqrt{\\frac{x-5}{2}}+1\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542127 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542080 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542127 1069 "q-162953" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542127 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542127 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{64{q}^{2}}}$ given that $q>0.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542127 1072 #uuid "21c2ef3e-e8f5-5baf-b96b-0dcf270605ef" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542127 1074 "First, we put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{64{q}^{2}}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{9}}{\\sqrt{64{q}^{2}}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{{3}^{2}}}{\\sqrt{64{q}^{2}}}=\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{64{q}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{64{q}^{2}}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{(8q)^{2}}} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{|8q|} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{|8|\\cdot |q|} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{8\\cdot |q|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we are told that $q>0,$ so we have $|q|=q.$ Therefore, the final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{8\\cdot |q|} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{8\\cdot q} & = \\\\ \\frac{3}{8q}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542128 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541992 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542128 1069 "q-121194" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542128 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542128 1071 "Consider the equation $2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-3=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, what is the numerical value of ${x}_{2}+{x}_{4}?$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542128 1072 #uuid "22f420b4-6e58-565d-a3ee-d0991eb0a624" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542128 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nFirst, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x-3 & =0 \\\\ 2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & =3 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & =\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡2x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & 2x & ={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{60}^{∘}}{2}+\\frac{n\\cdot {360}^{∘}}{2} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{{120}^{∘}}{2}+\\frac{n\\cdot {360}^{∘}}{2} \\\\ x & ={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={30}^{∘}$ and $x={60}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={210}^{∘}$ and $x={240}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx={30}^{∘},{60}^{∘},{210}^{∘},{240}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{2}={60}^{∘}$ and ${x}_{4}={240}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+{x}_{4}=60+240=300.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542129 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542002 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542129 1069 "q-8397" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542129 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542129 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ for $0\\le x<\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542129 1072 #uuid "2edac9cd-9abe-5210-bf19-134c059eeaf7" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542129 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & 2x+\\frac{\\pi }{3} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 2x & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+n\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{1}=0$ and ${x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0+\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542130 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542020 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542130 1069 "q-342789" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542130 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542130 1071 "The slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+2x}$ at the point where $x=1$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542130 1072 #uuid "316d8b52-05f5-5660-9987-6f2357a674ed" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542130 1074 "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nWe use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=-{x}^{2}+2x.$ Then $y=\\sqrt{u}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}},\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-2x+2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot (-2x+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{-2x+2}{2\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+2x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=1,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(\\frac{-2x+2}{2\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+2x}})|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2(1)+2}{2\\sqrt{-{1}^{2}+2(1)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{2\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542131 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542053 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542131 1069 "q-88012" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542131 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542131 1071 "Solve the inequality $3{log}_{3}⁡(x-1)-5<7.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542131 1072 #uuid "36e07a88-c6e0-5488-ba80-e3ecff483738" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542131 1074 "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{log}_{3}⁡(x-1)-5 & <7 \\\\ 3{log}_{3}⁡(x-1) & <12 \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(x-1) & <4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $3.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(x-1)} & <{3}^{4} \\\\ x-1 & <81 \\\\ x & <82\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx-1>0\\,⇒\\,x>1\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x<82,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,82).$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>1,$ which can be expressed as $(1,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,82)\\cap (1,\\infty )=(1,82)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (1,82),$ which can also be written as $1<x<82.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542132 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542021 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542132 1069 "q-134012" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542132 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542132 1071 "![](images/60/60b546c0e2d37362fefde1023ed093d2f544b187742bb690d5fe60bc417b48d4.png)\n\nWhat is the slope of the trend line shown above?" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542132 1072 #uuid "38830f71-0f2a-562f-b716-243d080e51d3" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542132 1074 "Notice that the trend line passes through the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(1,2)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(3,5).$\n\nTherefore, we compute the slope $m$ of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5-2}{3-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542133 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542093 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542133 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542110 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542133 1069 "q-266132" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542133 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542133 1071 "An angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane (measured in the usual way) has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={63}^{∘}.$ List, in ascending order, all the possible values of $\\theta $ in the range ${0}^{∘}\\le \\theta <{360}^{∘}.$\n\nIn ascending order, $\\theta $ can be equal to ${63}^{∘},$\n{{field-1}}\n${}^{∘},\\,\\,{243}^{∘},$ and\n{{field-2}}\n${}^{∘}.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542133 1072 #uuid "3a0d006a-6ec6-5d84-af8b-736e9b85507a" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542133 1074 "Let's remind ourselves of the relationship between ${\\theta }_{R}$ and $\\theta .$\n\n![](images/70/708bfa7367283d4be71b5df2c426862041b0a5ca939550e74f20e0e642856045.png)\n\nUsing ${\\theta }_{R}={63}^{∘},$ let's calculate all possible values of $\\theta .$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the first quadrant, then $\\theta ={\\theta }_{R}={63}^{∘}.$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the second quadrant, then $\\theta ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R}={180}^{∘}-{63}^{∘}={117}^{∘}.$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the third quadrant, then $\\theta ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R}={180}^{∘}+{63}^{∘}={243}^{∘}.$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the fourth quadrant, then $\\theta ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R}={360}^{∘}-{63}^{∘}={297}^{∘}.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, $\\theta $ can be equal to ${63}^{∘},$ ${117}^{∘},$ ${243}^{∘},$ and ${297}^{∘}.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542134 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542004 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542134 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542018 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542134 1069 "q-278633" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542134 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542134 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x-7)-1\\le \\operatorname{log}⁡(x+3).$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542134 1072 #uuid "3a94e199-2676-5231-8dfa-fab7d29de94f" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542134 1074 "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $10.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x-7)-1} & \\le {10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(x+3)} \\\\ {10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2x-7)}\\cdot {10}^{-1} & \\le {10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(x+3)} \\\\ (2x-7)\\cdot \\frac{1}{10} & \\le x+3 \\\\ 2x-7 & \\le 10x+30 \\\\ -8x & \\le 37 \\\\ x & \\ge -\\frac{37}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-7 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >\\frac{7}{2} \\\\ x+3 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\ge -\\frac{37}{8},$ which can be expressed as $[-\\frac{37}{8},\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>\\frac{7}{2}$ and $x>-3,$ which can be expressed as $(\\frac{7}{2},\\infty )$ and $(-3,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n[-\\frac{37}{8},\\infty )\\cap (\\frac{7}{2},\\infty )\\cap (-3,\\infty )=(\\frac{7}{2},\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (\\frac{7}{2},\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $x>\\frac{7}{2}.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542135 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542062 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542135 1069 "q-61969" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542135 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542135 1071 "$(x-3y)({x}^{2}+4y)=$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542135 1072 #uuid "3c104695-c936-5d0f-b4ab-74c8ede24e1a" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542135 1074 "We take each term of the first binomial and multiply it by the second binomial. Then, we simplify by collecting like terms.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3y)({x}^{2}+4y) & = \\\\ x({x}^{2}+4y)-3y({x}^{2}+4y) & = \\\\ {x}^{3}+4xy-3{x}^{2}y-12{y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe cannot simplify the above expression any further because there are no like terms." 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542136 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542017 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542136 1069 "q-119166" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542136 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542136 1071 "The volume of a cylinder decreases at a rate that's proportional to the square of its radius. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process. Assume that the height of the cylinder does not change and that $K>0.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542136 1072 #uuid "3da31f55-8d6c-5a19-aa68-60451055a7de" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542136 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of a cylinder **decreases** at a rate that's **proportional** to the **square of its radius**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a cylinder* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional to the square of its radius.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-k{r}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a cylinder is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ and the fact that the height of the cylinder does not change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\pi hr\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-k{r}^{2} \\\\ 2\\pi hr\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-k{r}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k{r}^{2}}{2\\pi hr} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{kr}{2\\pi h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{2\\pi h}>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-Kr.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542137 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542091 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542137 1069 "q-134007" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542137 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542137 1071 "![](images/41/41f5af0e317a35fb80eb33bfa0360b9f9b34a0d49a1a2b237e6f35a12d53b266.png)\n\nThe scatter plot above shows the relationship between the number of t-shirts and the number of socks sold by a clothing store over several days.\nWhich of the following is the trend line of the scatter plot above?" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542137 1072 #uuid "414bccf8-16e0-592d-9ff1-382b5feeaf1a" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542137 1074 "From the plot, it seems that the data points are scattered randomly. Therefore, there is no linear trend." 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542138 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542105 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542138 1069 "q-4101" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542138 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542138 1071 "Find $x+y$ if the midpoint of the line segment with endpoints $(x,y)$ and $(5,-4)$ is $(0,6).$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542138 1072 #uuid "43dcffb9-2ca6-59ab-b26c-63732a3440fb" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542138 1074 "The given points are ${{(x,y)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})}$, ${{(5,-4)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}$ and ${{(0,6)}_{⏟}}_{\\text{(}{x}_{m},\\,{y}_{m})}.$\n\nFirst, the $x$-coordinate of the midpoint's  must be the average of the $x$-coordinates of the endpoints: \n\n$$\n0=\\frac{x+5}{2}\\,⇒\\,x=-5.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the $y$-coordinate of the midpoint must be the average of the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints: \n\n$$\n6=\\frac{y+(-4)}{2}\\,⇒\\,y=16.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(x,y)=(-5,16)$ and $x+y=11.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542139 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541963 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542139 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541971 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542139 1069 "q-269517" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542139 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542139 1071 "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=(4Ax-5b)^{T}(4Ax-5b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$832$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542139 1072 #uuid "43f1cf78-e476-5744-a4e1-4eb640602151" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542139 1074 "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=4Ax-5b\\in {R}^{2}.$ Then, $L(A)={y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}({y}^{T}y)=2y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}=2{y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=4Ax-5b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}=4{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-5{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=4{x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot 4{x}_{j} \\\\  & =8{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is $8$ times the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=8y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =8y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =8(4Ax-5b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =8(4[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =8([\\begin{aligned}16 \\\\ -16\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 15\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}208 \\\\ -248\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 832 \\\\ 0 & -992\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542140 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542061 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542140 1069 "q-102985" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542140 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542140 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x-1)<\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5-4x).$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542140 1072 #uuid "43fb940b-f6bb-5ab7-9419-983a08453584" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542140 1074 "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x-1)} & <{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5-4x)} \\\\ 3x-1 & <5-4x \\\\ 7x & <6 \\\\ x & <\\frac{6}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-1 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 5-4x & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & <\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x<\\frac{6}{7},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,\\frac{6}{7}).$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>\\frac{1}{3}$ and $x<\\frac{5}{4},$ which can be expressed as $(\\frac{1}{3},\\infty )$ and $(-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{4}),$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{6}{7})\\cap (\\frac{1}{3},\\infty )\\cap (-\\infty ,\\frac{5}{4})=(\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{6}{7})\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (\\frac{1}{3},\\frac{6}{7}),$ which can also be written as $\\frac{1}{3}<x<\\frac{6}{7}.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542141 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541985 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542141 1069 "q-64577" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542141 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542141 1071 "![](images/a9/a9381ee6a4c0fb541f9459dd8c31fd994228c9cf6b42a2c4579f72cab96c37ae.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a unit circle. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $-0.7,$ which of the following is correct?" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542141 1072 #uuid "4900364d-1162-5845-8f68-07de4df04e97" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542141 1074 "![](images/fa/fab4d89dbe3697bb9e22b815939129bdb87f1919985edbf5c84628a1eb1d5853.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=-0.7$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =-0.7.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542142 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542001 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542142 1069 "q-75382" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542142 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542142 1071 "$3+\\frac{3}{2}x-1-\\frac{7}{4}x=$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542142 1072 #uuid "49e631ed-e489-57e1-8328-8ad962002fdf" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542142 1074 "We collect and combine the like terms, simplifying the coefficients as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3+\\frac{3x}{2}-1-\\frac{7x}{4} & = \\\\ (\\frac{3x}{2}-\\frac{7x}{4})+(3-1) & = \\\\ (\\frac{6}{4}-\\frac{7}{4})x+(3-1) & = \\\\ (\\frac{6-7}{4})x+2 & = \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4}x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542143 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542085 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542143 1069 "q-141356" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542143 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542143 1071 "Find the equation of the **normal** line to the polar curve $r={\\theta }^{2}$ at $\\theta =\\frac{3\\pi }{2},$ given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542143 1072 #uuid "4b160342-1c9b-5cb6-9f7b-8bf55c5af2c6" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542143 1074 "To find the equation of the normal line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})+\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}))}{(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cdot (-1)+\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0}{(2\\cdot 0-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\cdot (-1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{4}{3\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}^{2}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})}^{2}\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the normal:\n\nWe find the equation of the normal as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{m}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-(-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}) & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y+\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4} & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}\\,x \\\\ y & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}x-\\frac{9{\\pi }^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/8f/8f39669eaedc42a26d28d96f6dbb961080816e7fadd182b79a260c8de27b3d1a.png)" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542144 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542041 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542144 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542058 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542144 1069 "q-362903" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542144 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542144 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation $y=f(x)$ of the normal line to the polar curve $r=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the normal line is $y=$\n{{field-1}}$x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542144 1072 #uuid "4bd1b924-fea7-5666-9b66-85d3682d3092" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542144 1074 "To find the equation of the normal line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3} \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(\\sqrt{3},1).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the normal:\n\nWe find the equation of the normal as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{m}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}(x-\\sqrt{3}) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}x+1 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\,x+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/b2/b2485a1b1386468904947c52ff3f52997f72597da84384ae41d246f386a39232.png)" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542145 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542016 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542145 1069 "q-143140" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542145 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542145 1071 "At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **inversely** proportional to the shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis. The derivative at the point $(2,8)$ equals $5.$ What differential equation describes this curve?" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542145 1072 #uuid "500cc9b6-df77-5d16-8dd2-a74988fb81dd" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542145 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*At each point $P$ on a particular curve, the **derivative** ${y}^{′}(x)$ is **inversely proportional** to the **shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis**.*\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The *shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis* is given by $|x|.$\n\n- Since the *derivative* is *inversely proportional* to the shortest distance from $P$ to the $y$-axis, we have \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=k\\cdot \\frac{1}{|x|}\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant.\n\nSo, our differential equation is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{k}{|x|}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we're told that ${y}^{′}(x)=5$ when at the point $(2,8).$ We can use this to determine the value of the coefficient $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5=\\frac{k}{|2|}\\,⟹\\,k=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the differential equation describing the curve is\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(x)=\\frac{10}{|x|}.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542146 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541978 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542146 1069 "q-122681" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542146 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542146 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$  what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to $3$ decimal places?" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542146 1072 #uuid "50aa710d-f2c6-54ff-998e-2adb0b38d4dc" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542146 1074 "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{5\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{5\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-5 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-5 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =5 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(10)=1.471\\,127\\,674…≈1.471\\,128\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places. \n\nTo find the other solution in the given interval, we substract $\\pi $ from the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=1.471\\,128-\\pi ≈-1.670\\,465\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places. \n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈(1.471\\,128)\\cdot (-1.670\\,465)≈-2.457\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542147 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541999 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542147 1069 "q-70119" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542147 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542147 1071 "Evaluate $\\frac{3(2x-4)}{4(5-x)}$ for $x=6.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542147 1072 #uuid "5270a65a-9011-5c4a-8ec5-b8423546a5ab" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542147 1074 "We replace $x$ with $6$ in the expression and simplify as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3(2x-4)}{4(5-x)} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(2(6)-4)}{4(5-6)} & = \\\\ \\frac{3(8)}{4(-1)} & = \\\\ \\frac{24}{-4} & = \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542148 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542055 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542148 1069 "q-21088" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542148 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542148 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-{60}^{∘})=\\frac{1}{2}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542148 1072 #uuid "58a09865-e871-55ba-9b1a-dcc38d05fe6a" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542148 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-{60}^{∘} & ={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & x-{60}^{∘} & ={150}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our two equations for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={30}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={150}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={210}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={90}^{∘}$ and $x={210}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ So, the solutions are $x={90}^{∘},{210}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=90\\cdot 210=18\\,900.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542149 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542074 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542149 1069 "q-66873" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542149 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542149 1071 "Expressed in terms of common logarithms, what value of $x$ satisfies the equation ${10}^{x-2}={3}^{x}?$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542149 1072 #uuid "5c4b6334-f991-59a6-8939-03bf51753adb" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542149 1074 "First, we take the common logarithm $(log)$ of both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{10}^{x-2} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{x-2}) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({3}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx-2=\\operatorname{log}⁡({3}^{x})\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx-2=x\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =x\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) \\\\ x-x\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) & =2 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)) & =2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{1-\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542150 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542077 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542150 1069 "q-79363" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542150 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542150 1071 "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, what value of $x$ satisfies the equation ${e}^{x+2}=5\\cdot {6}^{x}?$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542150 1072 #uuid "5e9937d3-e10e-5251-81d5-87fad6e6c69a" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542150 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+2} & =5\\cdot {6}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x+2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5\\cdot {6}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx+2=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5\\cdot {6}^{x})\\,\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power and product laws of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({6}^{x}) \\\\ x+2 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ x-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-2 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-2}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542151 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542000 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542151 1069 "q-175589" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542151 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542151 1071 "If $\\frac{1}{15}=-\\frac{m}{3},$ then $m=${{field-1}}." 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542151 1072 #uuid "651d48b6-dcdd-5bc2-87b4-a66ffecf04ac" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542151 1074 "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n-\\frac{m}{3}=\\frac{1}{15}\n$$\n\nThen, we remove the negative sign from the variable. To do this, we multiply both sides of the equation by $-1.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{m}{3} & =\\frac{1}{15} \\\\ (-1)\\cdot (-\\frac{m}{3}) & =(-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{15} \\\\ \\frac{m}{3} & =-\\frac{1}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the equation above, $m$ is being divided by $3.$ To isolate $m$, we can perform the opposite operation, which is *multiplying* by $3.$ So, we multiply both sides of the equation by $3$ and simplify.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{m}{3} & =-\\frac{1}{15} \\\\ 3\\cdot \\frac{m}{3} & =3\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{15}) \\\\ \\frac{3m}{3} & =-\\frac{3}{15} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{3}m}{\\cancel{3}} & =-\\frac{1}{5} \\\\ m & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542152 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542033 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542152 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542036 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542152 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542037 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542152 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542066 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542152 1069 "q-279935" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542152 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542152 1071 "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-{60}^{∘})-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}=0$ for $-{90}^{∘}\\le x<{90}^{∘}.$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are written in **ascending order**, what are their values?\n\n${x}_{1}=${{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,$\n${x}_{2}=${{field-2}}${}^{∘},\\,$\n${x}_{3}=${{field-3}}${}^{∘},\\,$\n${x}_{4}=${{field-4}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542152 1072 #uuid "6785b9b0-7bde-5da0-a762-84192fbca0cb" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542152 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nFirst, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-{60}^{∘})-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-{60}^{∘}) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-{60}^{∘} & ={60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & 4x-{60}^{∘} & ={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & ={120}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & 4x & ={180}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $-{90}^{∘}\\le x<{90}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={30}^{∘}$ and $x={45}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=-1$ gives $x=-{60}^{∘}$ and $x=-{45}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $-{90}^{∘}\\le x<{90}^{∘}.$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=-{60}^{∘},-{45}^{∘},{30}^{∘},{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\nx={-60}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={-45}^{∘},\\,{x}_{3}={30}^{∘},\\,{x}_{4}={45}^{∘}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542153 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542051 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542153 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542086 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542153 1069 "q-330586" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542153 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542153 1071 "In **ascending order**, the exact solutions in radians to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542153 1072 #uuid "68c441f4-bd23-51b1-bd56-c0eea3474184" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542153 1074 "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\sqrt{3})=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is inside the given domain, one solution is ${x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo find another solution in the domain, we subtract $\\pi :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\pi =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542154 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542049 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542154 1069 "q-178632" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542154 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542154 1071 "The additive inverse of $24$ is{{field-1}}." 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542154 1072 #uuid "69122235-d824-5d29-8715-1c0104966b58" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542154 1074 "To find the additive inverse of the number $24$, we change its sign by placing a minus symbol $(-)$ in front of it. So, we obtain \n\n$$\n-\\,(24)=-24.\n$$\n\nTo check our work, we simply add the inverse to the original number. If we're correct, we should get zero.\n\n$$\n24+(-24)=24-24=0\\,✓\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542155 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542046 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542155 1069 "q-162933" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542155 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542155 1071 "$\\frac{\\sqrt{125{t}^{4}}}{\\sqrt{5}}=$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542155 1072 #uuid "6a46c4fe-4fbf-584a-9b57-cb0bbf472594" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542155 1074 "First, we bring the quotient under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{125{t}^{4}}}{\\sqrt{5}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{125{t}^{4}}{5}}=\\sqrt{25{t}^{4}}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{25{t}^{4}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(5{t}^{2})^{2}} & = \\\\ |5{t}^{2}| & = \\\\ |5|\\cdot |{t}^{2}| & = \\\\ 5\\cdot |{t}^{2}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot |{t}^{2}| & = \\\\ 5\\cdot {t}^{2} & = \\\\ 5{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542156 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542083 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542156 1069 "q-8376" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542156 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542156 1071 "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x+{30}^{∘})=-\\frac{1}{2}$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, what is the numerical value of ${x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}?$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542156 1072 #uuid "6b0a40ef-6c46-57c1-945d-c08da744533d" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542156 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+{30}^{∘} & =-{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & 2x+{30}^{∘} & ={210}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =-{60}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & 2x & ={180}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ x & =-{30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={90}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=-{30}^{∘}$ and $x={90}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={150}^{∘}$  and $x={270}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x={330}^{∘}$  and $x={450}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx={90}^{∘},{150}^{∘},{270}^{∘},{330}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{2}={150}^{∘}$ and ${x}_{3}={270}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{3}=150\\cdot 270=40\\,500.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542157 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542078 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542157 1069 "q-8837" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542157 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542157 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to $3$ decimal places?" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542157 1072 #uuid "6b7ee989-a51c-50ad-8da0-87452c898ca6" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542157 1074 "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+9\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{9\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{9\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 2\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+9 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+9 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-9 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\frac{9}{2})=-1.352\\,127\\,380\\,…≈-1.352\\,127\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places. \n\nSince $-1.352\\,127$ lies outside the given domain, it is not a solution.\nTo generate the solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =-1.352\\,127\\,380\\,…+\\pi =1.789\\,465\\,272\\,…≈1.789\\,465 \\\\ {x}_{2} & =-1.352\\,127\\,380\\,…+2\\pi =4.931\\,057\\,926\\,…≈4.931\\,058\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $6$ decimal places.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}≈1.789\\,466\\cdot 4.931\\,058≈8.824\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542158 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542063 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542158 1069 "q-100186" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542158 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542158 1071 "Simplify $(-32)^{0}.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542158 1072 #uuid "6c864496-70bc-5f2d-82d0-559a3bc9072f" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542158 1074 "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1.$ So, \n\n$$\n(-32)^{0}=1.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542159 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542104 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542159 1069 "q-64174" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542159 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542159 1071 "![](images/2d/2d3fc4b568492c8435f72c28010c21b0c0746e2abe46922fdb942eaba5cf8b05.png)\n\nWhat $x:y$ ratio is shown in the graph above?" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542159 1072 #uuid "6ca180bb-426b-5acf-99a3-c4512087f7c5" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542159 1074 "We see that the point $(7,5)$ lies on the graph. Let's write the ratio $7:5$ as a fraction:\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{5}\n$$\n\nWe can not simplify this fraction further. Hence, we can say that the graph shows the ratio $7:5.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542160 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541981 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542160 1069 "q-242900" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542160 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542160 1071 "Given that ${5}^{x+4}={2}^{2-x},$ what is the value of $x?$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542160 1072 #uuid "7088fa93-cf81-5263-ba93-771873a7af12" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542160 1074 "First, we take ${log}_{5}$ on both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{x+4} & ={2}^{2-x} \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡({5}^{x+4}) & ={log}_{5}⁡({2}^{2-x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nx+4={log}_{5}⁡({2}^{2-x})\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx+4=(2-x){log}_{5}⁡(2)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & =(2-x){log}_{5}⁡(2) \\\\ x+4 & =2{log}_{5}⁡(2)-x{log}_{5}⁡(2) \\\\ x+x{log}_{5}⁡(2) & =2{log}_{5}⁡(2)-4 \\\\ x(1+{log}_{5}⁡(2)) & =2{log}_{5}⁡(2)-4 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2{log}_{5}⁡(2)-4}{1+{log}_{5}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the change of base formula, we have\n\n$$\n{log}_{5}⁡(2)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}≈0.430\\,677.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $x$ as\n\n$$\nx≈\\frac{2\\cdot 0.430\\,677-4}{1+0.430\\,677}≈-2.194,\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542161 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542099 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542161 1069 "q-100470" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542161 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542161 1071 "Find the solution of ${5}^{x+3}={2}^{5-4x}.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542161 1072 #uuid "708af368-563d-5975-a829-b0da728a3e85" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542161 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{x+3} & ={2}^{5-4x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{x+3}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{5-4x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =(5-4x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =(5-4x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)) & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x & =\\frac{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542162 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541995 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542162 1069 "q-222054" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542162 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542162 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=4{x}^{2}-40x+15$ for $x\\ge 5.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542162 1072 #uuid "79783b94-bb53-5240-8c0c-52c93a581d6b" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542162 1074 "First, we complete the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =4{x}^{2}-40x+15 \\\\  & =4({x}^{2}-10x)+15 \\\\  & =4({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 5x+{5}^{2}-{5}^{2})+15 \\\\  & =4((x-5)^{2}-25)+15 \\\\  & =4(x-5)^{2}-100+15 \\\\  & =4(x-5)^{2}-85\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTo find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny=4(x-5)^{2}-85\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx=4(y-5)^{2}-85\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =4(y-5)^{2}-85 \\\\ x+85 & =4(y-5)^{2} \\\\ \\frac{x+85}{4} & =(y-5)^{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{x+85}{4}} & =y-5 \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{x+85}}{2}+5 & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we selected the positive square root because if $x\\ge 5$ is the domain of $f,$ then the range of the inverse is ${f}^{-1}\\ge 5.$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{\\sqrt{x+85}}{2}+5\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542163 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542073 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542163 1069 "q-46409" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542163 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542163 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=-{x}^{2}+2x+5$ for $x\\ge 1.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542163 1072 #uuid "87d49f48-029f-525c-b4c7-c630bf956527" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542163 1074 "First, we complete the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =-{x}^{2}+2x+5 \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-2x)+5 \\\\  & =-({x}^{2}-2\\cdot x+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2})+5 \\\\  & =-((x-1)^{2}-1)+5 \\\\  & =-(x-1)^{2}+1+5 \\\\  & =-(x-1)^{2}+6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTo find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny=-(x-1)^{2}+6\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx=-(y-1)^{2}+6\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-(y-1)^{2}+6 \\\\ x-6 & =-(y-1)^{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{-x+6} & =y-1 \\\\ \\sqrt{-x+6}+1 & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we selected the positive square root because if $x\\ge 1$ is the domain of $f,$ then the range of the inverse is ${f}^{-1}\\ge 1.$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\sqrt{-x+6}+1\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542164 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541998 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542164 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542107 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542164 1069 "q-266135" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542164 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542164 1071 "An angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane (measured in the usual way) has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={15}^{∘}.$ List, in ascending order, all the possible values of $\\theta $ in the range ${0}^{∘}\\le \\theta <{360}^{∘}.$\n\nIn ascending order, $\\theta $ can be equal to ${15}^{∘},$\n{{field-1}}\n${}^{∘},\\,$\n{{field-2}}\n${}^{∘},$ and\n$\\,\\,{345}^{∘},$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542164 1072 #uuid "8a8a6201-2066-5328-adab-169a430a54b6" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542164 1074 "Let's remind ourselves of the relationship between ${\\theta }_{R}$ and $\\theta .$\n\n![](images/48/4891c7eea304bff2598ba5b19118951183b196eb4f2f4249573706b6625066e2.png)\n\nUsing ${\\theta }_{R}={15}^{∘},$ let's calculate all possible values of $\\theta .$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the first quadrant, then $\\theta ={\\theta }_{R}={15}^{∘}.$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the second quadrant, then $\\theta ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R}={180}^{∘}-{15}^{∘}={165}^{∘}.$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the third quadrant, then $\\theta ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R}={180}^{∘}+{15}^{∘}={195}^{∘}.$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the fourth quadrant, then $\\theta ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R}={360}^{∘}-{15}^{∘}={345}^{∘}.$\n\nTherefore, in ascending order, $\\theta $ can be equal to ${15}^{∘},$ ${165}^{∘},$ ${195}^{∘},$ and ${345}^{∘}.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542165 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542060 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542165 1069 "q-242905" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542165 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542165 1071 "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${e}^{x-3}=5\\cdot {8}^{2x}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542165 1072 #uuid "8c789237-199d-5f09-befa-8f93e41fb414" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542165 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-3} & =5\\cdot {8}^{2x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x-3}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5\\cdot {8}^{2x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx-3=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5\\cdot {8}^{2x}).\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power and product laws of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({8}^{2x}) \\\\ x-3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ x-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) & =3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x(1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)) & =3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x & =\\frac{3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542166 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542054 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542166 1069 "q-156073" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542166 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542166 1071 "What is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{262}^{∘}$ expressed in terms of a reference angle?" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542166 1072 #uuid "8c9c8f3d-e984-5471-bc82-27a595032835" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542166 1074 "To express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{262}^{∘}$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\nFirst, let's draw the angle ${262}^{∘}$ in the coordinate plane (CAST diagram):\n\n![](images/b6/b64a679dea08fe6472b146e0ae43cbfbc01577ef019619c3c44c6e22000c7c61.png)\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta ={262}^{∘}$ is in the $3$rd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\theta -{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={262}^{∘}-{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={82}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{262}^{∘}$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{82}^{∘}.$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{262}^{∘}$ must be *negative* because the sine ratio is *always* negative in the $3$rd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{262}^{∘}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{82}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542167 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542064 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542167 1069 "q-143069" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542167 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542167 1071 "What is the inverse of $f(x)=8-3{x}^{2}$ for $x\\ge 0?$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542167 1072 #uuid "8ea93b33-07b4-5d80-ad03-66a9bad29401" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542167 1074 "To find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny=8-3{x}^{2}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx=8-3{y}^{2}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =8-3{y}^{2} \\\\ x-8 & =-3{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{8-x}{3} & ={y}^{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{\\frac{8-x}{3}} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we selected the positive square root because if $x\\ge 0$ is the domain of $f,$ then the range of the inverse is ${f}^{-1}\\ge 0.$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\sqrt{\\frac{8-x}{3}}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542168 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541975 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542168 1069 "q-134029" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542168 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542168 1071 "![](images/e2/e2154f7bdd31e0c35517051fe46764ab75e597d0d78dbc64ffc55d1faecbec78.png)\n\nEstimate the equation of the trend line for the scatter plot above. Round both the slope and the $y$-intercept to the nearest $0.5.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542168 1072 #uuid "8f2a8490-6351-51db-83b8-81572c8b1d30" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542168 1074 "First, let's draw an approximate trend line for our scatter plot:\n\n![](images/68/687b257b9e2c6d3d2a015a5703d4fc29e9ab0598fb7e7ac69b588334c4a1e73d.png)\n\nIt seems that the trend line passes through $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})≈(2,4)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})≈(6,6).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we estimate the slope $m$ of the line as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ≈\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-4}{6-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=0.5$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=0.5x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to estimate the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can do this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(2,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =0.5x+b \\\\ 4 & =0.5(2)+b \\\\ 4 & =1+b \\\\ 3 & =b \\\\ b & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=0.5x+3.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542169 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542012 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542169 1069 "q-8877" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542169 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542169 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions of the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ for $-\\pi <x\\le \\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542169 1072 #uuid "9106b848-9f77-574a-a8bb-ee8c8855bad8" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542169 1074 "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =-\\sqrt{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo find the other solution, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3}=-\\frac{2{\\pi }^{2}}{9}.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542170 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542010 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542170 1069 "q-215792" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542170 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542170 1071 "If $p=-1,$ then $|4p+1{|}^{3}=${{field-1}}." 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542170 1072 #uuid "9169a9eb-58fd-54ef-9fd7-4f2515dd156b" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542170 1074 "We substitute $p=-1$ and then evaluate the absolute value before we simplify the rest of the expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|4p+1{|}^{3} & = \\\\ |4(-1)+1{|}^{3} & = \\\\ |-4+1{|}^{3} & = \\\\ |-3{|}^{3} & = \\\\ (3)^{3} & = \\\\ 27\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542171 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541996 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542171 1069 "q-134020" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542171 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542171 1071 "![](images/b8/b82ed81f724322dc05a2c65d3635ac74f5ca92ff413e9d7e5131ebb604269f26.png)\n\nDetermine the equation of the trend line shown above." 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542171 1072 #uuid "94bd2cbd-504c-548c-9ed1-5e5adaadef48" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542171 1074 "Notice that the trend line passes through the points $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-4,6)$ and $({x}_{2},{y}_{2})=(4,3).$\n\nWe will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form \n\n$$\ny=mx+b.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the slope $m$ of the line, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3-6}{4-(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $m=-\\frac{3}{8}$ into the slope-intercept form above, we get\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{3}{8}x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(-4,6):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{3}{8}x+b \\\\ 6 & =-\\frac{3}{8}(-4)+b \\\\ 6 & =\\frac{3}{2}+b \\\\ 12 & =3+2b \\\\ b & =\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is \n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{3}{8}x+\\frac{9}{2}.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542172 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542084 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542172 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542098 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542172 1069 "q-330574" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542172 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542172 1071 "In **ascending order**, the exact solutions in radians to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0,$ for $-\\pi \\le x<\\pi ,$ are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542172 1072 #uuid "95e3e644-e5ac-51bf-963d-8477ce790acd" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542172 1074 "We divide each term of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\sqrt{3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =-\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(-\\sqrt{3})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ is inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo generate the solutions, we add integer multiples of $\\pi .$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542173 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541974 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542173 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542013 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542173 1069 "q-278513" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542173 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542173 1071 "Solve the inequality $5{log}_{2}⁡(x+4)-2>0.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542173 1072 #uuid "96832f47-df31-501c-8b04-84adf76c5b92" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542173 1074 "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5{log}_{2}⁡(x+4)-2 & >0 \\\\ 5{log}_{2}⁡(x+4) & >2 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(x+4) & >\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $2.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(x+4)} & >{2}^{2/5} \\\\ x+4 & >\\sqrt[5]{4} \\\\ x & >\\sqrt[5]{4}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx+4>0\\,⇒\\,x>-4\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x>\\sqrt[5]{4}-4,$ which can be expressed as $(\\sqrt[5]{4}-4,\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>-4,$ which can be expressed as $(-4,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(\\sqrt[5]{4}-4,\\infty )\\cap (-4,\\infty )=(\\sqrt[5]{4}-4,\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is \n\n$$\nx\\in (\\sqrt[5]{4}-4,\\infty ),\\text{ which can also be written as }x>\\sqrt[5]{4}-4.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542174 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541979 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542174 1069 "q-146287" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542174 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542174 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{27}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ |\n| $X=9$ | $\\frac{5}{27}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=3]$ given that the random variables $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{X|3}=\\frac{22}{3}.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542174 1072 #uuid "9a9df102-8e4d-542b-ad62-072cc2469e7e" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542174 1074 "Recall that the conditional variance of $X$ given $Y=y$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=y] & =\\text{E}[(X-{\\mu }_{X|y})^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}\\,|\\,Y=y]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)]-{\\mu }_{X|y}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y),$ let's first find the marginal distribution of $Y,$ corresponding to the column totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{27}$ | $\\frac{2}{9}$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ |\n| $X=9$ | $\\frac{5}{27}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| ${f}_{Y}(y)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{2}{3}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{Y}(y)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding column total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $X$ given that $Y=y:$\n\n| ${f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,y)$ | $Y=3$ | $Y=9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=3$ | $\\frac{1}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| $X=6$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{6}$ |\n| $X=9$ | $\\frac{5}{9}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n|  | $1$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X\\,|\\,Y=3] & =[{\\sum }_{x}{x}^{2}\\,{f}_{X|Y}(x\\,|\\,3)]-{\\mu }_{X|3}^{2} \\\\  & =[{3}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}+{6}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}+{9}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{5}{9}]-{(\\frac{22}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =1+12+45-\\frac{484}{9} \\\\  & =\\frac{38}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542175 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542081 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542175 1069 "q-102984" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542175 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542175 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-x)>\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-7).$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542175 1072 #uuid "9adad641-ba08-51a7-99da-8781356ee913" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542175 1074 "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2-x)} & >{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-7)} \\\\ 2-x & >x-7 \\\\ -2x & >-9 \\\\ x & <\\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-x & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & <2 \\\\ x-7 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x<\\frac{9}{2},$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,\\frac{9}{2}).$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x<2$ and $x>7,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,2)$ and $(7,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,\\frac{9}{2})\\cap (-\\infty ,2)\\cap (7,\\infty )=∅\n$$\n\nThe intersection is empty. Therefore, there is no solution." 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542176 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542108 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542176 1069 "q-266102" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542176 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542176 1071 "![](images/ee/ee43228323c28f8ed93364b02dd6a5e8984f0f92a8697ad0fa68e7ae16f82f27.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a unit circle and the angle $\\theta $ measured in the usual way. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $-0.64,$ calculate the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ to the nearest integer.\n\n${\\theta }_{R}≈$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542176 1072 #uuid "9c4fbf40-0d65-50a7-8d6d-c2f0eae5194f" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542176 1074 "The reference angle is the acute angle that $\\theta $ makes with the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, we create a right-triangle by drawing a vertical line from $P$ to the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/6f/6fdb497f69777c364f464e0ebd7ac4d4884679a3a38956da2818866d886614d2.png)\n\nSo, we have the following triangle, where ${\\theta }_{R}$ is the reference angle of $\\theta .$\n\n![](images/98/9862a9d72f39e7f6d9abd861e4c3078cb67962d50548fae8ee98a634f262fed6.png)\n\nWe know that the hypotenuse has a length of $1$ because it is a radius of the unit circle. \n\nNow, using the triangle above, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{0.64}{1}=0.64.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ using the inverse sine:\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0.64)≈{40}^{∘}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542177 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541977 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542177 1069 "q-100187" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542177 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542177 1071 "Simplify $(-17)^{0}.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542177 1072 #uuid "9ddda73a-66ad-5c78-97bd-db73c90fdc26" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542177 1074 "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1.$ So, \n\n$$\n(-17)^{0}=1.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542178 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541984 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542178 1069 "q-246855" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542178 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542178 1071 "![](images/1d/1d6ba20a6e68add48e3d979971c819a8d45a5ec8473a6781a6f88797af442328.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the angle $\\theta ={200}^{∘}$ in the coordinate plane.  What is its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}?$\n\n${\\theta }_{R}=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542178 1072 #uuid "9f1700e6-8df7-5af8-96a1-8f4f67997ae9" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542178 1074 "![](images/7e/7e7ddf9e0a6edbfd5521ca5de1d718ab9c66b7e0a96ed8d52798fd16daf63aa5.png)\n\nThe reference angle, denoted ${\\theta }_{R},$  is the angle that $\\theta ={200}^{∘}$ makes with the $x$-axis.\n\nAs $\\theta $ is in the third quadrant, its reference angle is given by\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}=\\theta -{180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}={200}^{∘}-{180}^{∘}={20}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542179 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542015 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542179 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542109 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542179 1069 "q-269429" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542179 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542179 1071 "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=\\frac{1}{2}(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]?$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$9$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-6$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542179 1072 #uuid "a276eb8e-dc20-5069-8231-5e2d2d2a281a" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542179 1074 "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b\\in {R}^{2}.$ Then, $L(A)=\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}(\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{T}y)=y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}={y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=Ax-b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}={x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & ={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =(Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =([\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =([\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}])\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 9 \\\\ -6 & -9\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542180 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541987 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542180 1069 "q-119170" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542180 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542180 1071 "The volume of a cylinder decreases at a rate proportional to its volume. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the height, $h(t),$ in this process. \nAssume that the radius of the cylinder does not change and that $k>0.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542180 1072 #uuid "a29a3c61-aa9f-590b-908a-6227844310f9" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542180 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **volume** of a cylinder **decreases** at a rate **proportional** to its **volume**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *volume $V$ of a cylinder* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate *proportional to the volume.* Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t}=-kV,\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe volume $V$ of a cylinder is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {r}^{2}h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ and using the fact that the radius of the cylinder does not change, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =\\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}V}{\\text{d}t} & =-kV \\\\ \\pi {r}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-k(\\pi {r}^{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k\\pi {r}^{2}h}{\\pi {r}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-kh.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542181 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541988 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542181 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542047 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542181 1069 "q-276949" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542181 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542181 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+4)-6>0.$\n\n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542181 1072 #uuid "a424e3b7-6a84-5fcd-baa0-a03ad7cec111" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542181 1074 "First, we isolate the logarithm:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+4)-6 & >0 \\\\ \\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+4) & >6 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+4) & >10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $e.$ We get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+4)} & >{e}^{10} \\\\ x+4 & >{e}^{10} \\\\ x & >{e}^{10}-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithm, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\nx+4>0\\,⇒\\,x>-4\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x>{e}^{10}-4,$ which can be expressed as $({e}^{10}-4,\\infty ).$ Additionally, the domain of the logarithm is $x>-4,$ which can be expressed as $(-4,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithm, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n({e}^{10}-4,\\infty )\\cap (-4,\\infty )=({e}^{10}-4,\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in ({e}^{10}-4,\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $x>{e}^{10}-4.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542182 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542082 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542182 1069 "q-143132" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542182 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542182 1071 "The area of a circle decreases at a rate that's proportional to the cube of its radius. Write down a differential equation describing the rate of change of the radius, $r(t),$ in this process. Assume that $K>0.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542182 1072 #uuid "a8d62c5b-3e1c-59af-b194-160b1241f14c" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542182 1074 "Let's highlight the important words in the given statement:\n\n*The **area** of a circle **decreases** at a **rate** that's **proportional** to the cube of its **radius**. *\n\nWe note the following:\n\n- The question describes the *rate* at which the *area $A$ of a circle* varies. Therefore, this is a statement about the quantity \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\n- We're told that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ *decreases* at a rate that's *proportional* to the cube of its *radius.* In other words,\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-k{r}^{3}\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0.$\n\nWe wish to use this information to find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nThe area of a circle is given by \n\n$$\nA=\\pi {r}^{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nDifferentiating this equation with respect to $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi {r}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-k{r}^{3} \\\\ 2\\pi r\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-k{r}^{3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k{r}^{3}}{2\\pi r} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{k{r}^{2}}{2\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now introduce a new constant:\n\n$$\nK=\\frac{k}{2\\pi }>0\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, our equation becomes\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t}=-K{r}^{2}.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542183 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541990 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542183 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541997 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542183 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542038 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542183 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542044 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542183 1069 "q-279860" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542183 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542183 1071 "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{1}{2}$ for $0\\le x<\\pi .$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are written in **ascending order**, what are their values? Express your answers in terms of $\\pi .$\n\n${x}_{1}=${{field-1}}$,\\,$\n${x}_{2}=${{field-2}}$,\\,$\n${x}_{3}=${{field-3}}$,\\,$\n${x}_{4}=${{field-4}}" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542183 1072 #uuid "a9a4a2f3-242d-5c49-b478-bafd32239530" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542183 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & 4x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 4x & =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi  \\\\ 4x & =\\frac{17\\pi }{12}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 4x & =\\frac{25\\pi }{12}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{17\\pi }{48}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{25\\pi }{48}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{17\\pi }{48}+\\frac{24n\\pi }{48} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{25\\pi }{48}+\\frac{24n\\pi }{48} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(24n+17)\\pi }{48} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{(24n+25)\\pi }{48}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{17\\pi }{48}$ and $x=\\frac{25\\pi }{48}$\n\n- Setting $n=-1$ gives $x=-\\frac{7\\pi }{48}$ (too small) and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{48}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{41\\pi }{48}$ and $x=\\frac{49\\pi }{48}$ (too big)\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{48},\\frac{17\\pi }{48},\\frac{25\\pi }{48},\\frac{41\\pi }{48}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{48},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{17\\pi }{48},\\,{x}_{3}=\\frac{25\\pi }{48},\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{41\\pi }{48}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542184 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541980 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542184 1069 "q-134003" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542184 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542184 1071 "![](images/44/442b69f47d93a193edb5cae73f01687986c27601a7dabf047745af7c52717b46.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the trend line of the scatter plot above?" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542184 1072 #uuid "aa6f1856-5e72-5fda-93a1-cb8216322507" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542184 1074 "The plot shows that the data points are scattered along a straight line.\n\n![](images/15/155fe3102a11f4b2d17dd9260721d03941c4c571dbf64929e056436dc1264d58.png)\n\nWe see that the line $r$ best fits the given data set." 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542185 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541982 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542185 1069 "q-156608" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542185 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542185 1071 "What is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{173}^{∘}$ expressed in terms of a reference angle?" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542185 1072 #uuid "ace8cb21-d5d1-5afe-a9db-0af7bfa7a631" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542185 1074 "To express $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{173}^{∘}$ in terms of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\nFirst, let's draw the angle ${173}^{∘}$ in the coordinate plane (CAST diagram):\n\n![](images/bb/bb5ea51c0fabeb994c161a53ed5bdc0869c988eb8ab60993e7e1fe96a058040f.png)\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta ={173}^{∘}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & ={180}^{∘}-\\theta  \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{173}^{∘} \\\\  & ={7}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{173}^{∘}$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡{7}^{∘}.$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{173}^{∘}$ must be *negative* because the tangent ratio is *always* negative in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{173}^{∘}=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{7}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542186 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542072 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542186 1069 "q-162925" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542186 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542186 1071 "$\\sqrt{\\frac{9{b}^{4}}{16}}=$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542186 1072 #uuid "ad438bd0-4fe5-5891-83d5-f58699093a97" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542186 1074 "First, we put the numerator and denominator under separate radicals and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{\\frac{9{b}^{4}}{16}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{9{b}^{4}}}{\\sqrt{16}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{9{b}^{4}}}{\\sqrt{{4}^{2}}}=\\frac{\\sqrt{9{b}^{4}}}{4}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\sqrt{9{b}^{4}}}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{(3{b}^{2})^{2}}}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{|3{b}^{2}|}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{|3|\\cdot |{b}^{2}|}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\cdot |{b}^{2}|}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3\\cdot |{b}^{2}|}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{3\\cdot {b}^{2}}{4} & = \\\\ \\frac{3{b}^{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542187 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542048 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542187 1069 "q-266076" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542187 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542187 1071 "![](images/64/6427becc41fd736c4161fd41fce92e76352315fd535132c1c8804b5d0949587e.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a unit circle and the angle $\\theta $ measured in the usual way. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $-0.26,$ calculate the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ to the nearest integer.\n\n${\\theta }_{R}≈$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542187 1072 #uuid "ad8c8e6a-2931-50af-b1e7-7872437ed775" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542187 1074 "The reference angle is the acute angle that $\\theta $ makes with the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, we create a right-triangle by drawing a vertical line from $P$ to the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/40/403edda1121bb4a647a52fcff755659ab1f9fe2ea310572b13be423cef4d6db9.png)\n\nSo, we have the following triangle, where ${\\theta }_{R}$ is the reference angle of $\\theta .$\n\n![](images/38/38a60a712b18a4b9575ed812f8bd184a56ae5969fe865b9edc12f2b0faccd48f.png)\n\nWe know that the hypotenuse has a length of $1$ because it is a radius of the unit circle. \n\nNow, using the triangle above, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{0.26}{1}=0.26.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ using the inverse cosine:\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.26)≈{75}^{∘}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542188 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542003 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542188 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542009 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542188 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542092 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542188 1069 "q-278632" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542188 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542188 1071 "Solve the inequality $\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x-4)\\le \\operatorname{log}⁡(x+8).$\n\n{{field-1}}$<\\,x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542188 1072 #uuid "adae2d29-07f7-5943-8df9-e670e02b37d2" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542188 1074 "We use each side of the inequality as an exponent of $10.$ This gives the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x-4)} & \\le {10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(x+8)} \\\\ {10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3x-4)} & \\le {10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(x+8)} \\\\ 3x-4 & \\le x+8 \\\\ 2x & \\le 12 \\\\ x & \\le 6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the logarithms in the original inequality. To find the domain of the logarithms, we recall that the argument of the logarithm must be positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-4 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ x+8 & >0 & ⇒ &  & x & >-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\le 6,$ which can be expressed as $(-\\infty ,6].$ Additionally, the domains of the logarithms are $x>\\frac{4}{3}$ and $x>-8,$ which can be expressed as $(\\frac{4}{3},\\infty )$ and $(-8,\\infty )$ respectively. To find the solutions that are in the domains of the logarithms, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(-\\infty ,6]\\cap (\\frac{4}{3},\\infty )\\cap (-8,\\infty )=(\\frac{4}{3},6]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (\\frac{4}{3},6],$ which can also be written as $\\frac{4}{3}<x\\le 6.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542189 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542026 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542189 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542028 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542189 1069 "q-330502" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542189 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542189 1071 "In **ascending order**,  the exact solutions in radians to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ are\n\n${x}_{1}=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542189 1072 #uuid "adbef047-215a-508f-b02c-be145edbac02" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542189 1074 "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the other solution in the given interval, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\pi =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, in ascending order, the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542190 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542050 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542190 1069 "q-269381" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542190 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542190 1071 "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=2({y}^{T}Ax)$ where \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}10 & 10 \\\\ -5 & -5\\end{aligned}]?$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542190 1072 #uuid "ae4e117c-08e3-5ee2-be97-840ec47e6061" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542190 1074 "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWriting out $L$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\nL={\\sum }_{i=1}^{2}{\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{y}_{i}{x}_{j},\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${∇}_{A}L=2(y\\cdot {x}^{T}).$\n\nEvaluating at the given values $x=[\\begin{aligned}9 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ and $y=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],$ we get\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=2([\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}9 & 0\\end{aligned}])=2([\\begin{aligned}9 & 0 \\\\ -9 & 0\\end{aligned}])=[\\begin{aligned}18 & 0 \\\\ -18 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542191 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542031 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542191 1069 "q-170401" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542191 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542191 1071 "The maximum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=-4{x}_{1}^{2}+6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $5.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542191 1072 #uuid "afcfe65d-5017-5ca6-9a0c-f6939bdd2a4f" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542191 1074 "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 4\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-9 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542192 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541991 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542192 1069 "q-8377" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542192 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542192 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$ for $0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{x}_{2}?$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542192 1072 #uuid "afef3ba2-5b32-54ef-ad36-e24901c18733" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542192 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & 3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 3x & =2\\pi +2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{3} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi (4n+3)}{6} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{2(n+1)\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ (too big)\n\n- Setting $n=-1$ gives $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ (too small) and $x=0.$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ are\n\n$$\nx=0,\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=0+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542193 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542011 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542193 1069 "q-156598" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542193 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542193 1071 "What is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{280}^{∘}$ expressed in terms of a reference angle?" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542193 1072 #uuid "b0f9ed04-9025-5f06-a930-77e84f985c93" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542193 1074 "To express $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{280}^{∘}$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\nFirst, let's draw the angle ${280}^{∘}$ in the coordinate plane (CAST diagram):\n\n![](images/2a/2ad04709282b00c6a185891c219017cbfb55af57845a7cfa28d1f1cc4b677c58.png)\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta ={280}^{∘}$ is in the $4$th quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & ={360}^{∘}-\\theta  \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{280}^{∘} \\\\  & ={80}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{280}^{∘}$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{80}^{∘}.$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{280}^{∘}$ must be *positive* because the cosine ratio is *always* positive in the $4$th quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡{280}^{∘}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{80}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542194 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542102 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542194 1069 "q-143057" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542194 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542194 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-12x+13$ for $x\\le 2.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542194 1072 #uuid "b5d4ec46-41de-5071-92d0-61a853c21e80" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542194 1074 "First, we complete the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =3{x}^{2}-12x+13 \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}-4x)+13 \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x)+13 \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}-{2}^{2})+13 \\\\  & =3((x-2)^{2}-4)+13 \\\\  & =3(x-2)^{2}-12+13 \\\\  & =3(x-2)^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTo find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=3(x-2)^{2}+1\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx=3(y-2)^{2}+1\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3(y-2)^{2}+1 \\\\ 3(y-2)^{2} & =x-1 \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =\\frac{x-1}{3} \\\\ y-2 & =-\\sqrt{\\frac{x-1}{3}} \\\\ y & =2-\\sqrt{\\frac{x-1}{3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we selected the negative square root because if the domain of $f(x)$ is $x\\le 2,$ then the range of ${f}^{-1}(x)$ is  ${f}^{-1}\\le 2.$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=2-\\sqrt{\\frac{x-1}{3}}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542025 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542195 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542029 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542195 1069 "q-279990" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542195 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542195 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ in **ascending order**, what are the values of ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}?$ Express your answers in terms of $\\pi .$\n\n${x}_{1}=${{field-1}}$,\\,$\n${x}_{2}=${{field-2}}" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542195 1072 #uuid "b6f8b9f9-43bb-5fdf-8d90-07de7dc4a372" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542195 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & \\frac{x}{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving our two equations for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & \\frac{x}{3} & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  \\\\ \\frac{x}{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & \\frac{x}{3} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+6n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+6n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{12n\\pi }{2} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}+\\frac{12n\\pi }{2} \\\\ x & =\\frac{(12n+1)\\pi }{2} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{(12n+3)\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions are: \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542196 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542056 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542196 1069 "q-64583" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542196 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542196 1071 "![](images/9a/9a4b6e20d8ab249f0aab42f4c21cd9b8e2bf9c04fc34ba3462aa07175d797af9.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a unit circle. Given that the $y$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $-0.6,$ which of the following is correct?" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542196 1072 #uuid "b7061407-4d39-5b42-8242-7b789dd439ff" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542196 1074 "![](images/b2/b269afd0b8fb3b82423a2f80b3dfb5aa39aeeea26d93d6d3220488456d310248.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=-0.6$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =-0.6.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542197 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542032 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542197 1069 "q-66883" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542197 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542197 1071 "Expressed in terms of common logarithms, what value of $x$ satisfies the equation ${10}^{x+1}={3}^{x+2}?$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542197 1072 #uuid "b722f56c-7d4f-5883-9de4-4bfad6f8a324" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542197 1074 "First, we take the common logarithm $(log)$ of both sides of the equation:   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{10}^{x+1} & ={3}^{x+2} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{x+1}) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({3}^{x+2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx+1=\\operatorname{log}⁡({3}^{x+2})\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =(x+2)\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) \\\\ x+1 & =x\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =x\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) \\\\ x-x\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) & =2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)-1 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)) & =2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)-1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)-1}{1-\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542198 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542094 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542198 1069 "q-351197" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542198 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542198 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation $y=f(x)$ of the tangent line to the polar curve $r={e}^{-\\theta }$ at $\\theta =0,$ given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the tangent line is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542198 1072 #uuid "b816908c-853d-5e9e-8dd6-8428f48a97f8" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542198 1074 "To find the equation of the tangent line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =0} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡0-\\operatorname{cos}⁡0}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡0+\\operatorname{sin}⁡0} \\\\  & =\\frac{0-1}{1+0} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0 \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(1,0).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent:\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-1(x-1) \\\\ y & =-x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/66/6651277bec90a77e19d0bd6c1d1a464f636cf675cae25ad47a6f60a2a352002b.png)" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542199 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542042 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542199 1069 "q-222055" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542199 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542199 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)={x}^{2}-12x+40$ for $x\\ge 6.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542199 1072 #uuid "b957784b-0ac4-5ba4-bc18-3ca9998ce9ae" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542199 1074 "First, we complete the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{2}-12x+40 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2})-{6}^{2}+40 \\\\  & =(x-6)^{2}-36+40 \\\\  & =(x-6)^{2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTo find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny=(x-6)^{2}+4\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx=(y-6)^{2}+4\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(y-6)^{2}+4 \\\\ x-4 & =(y-6)^{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{x-4} & =y-6 \\\\ \\sqrt{x-4}+6 & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we selected the positive square root because if $x\\ge 6$ is the domain of $f,$ then the range of the inverse is ${f}^{-1}\\ge 6.$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\sqrt{x-4}+6\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542200 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542090 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542200 1069 "q-8375" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542200 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542200 1071 "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{1}{2}$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are written in ascending order, what is ${x}_{2}+{x}_{3}?$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542200 1072 #uuid "b9773b0d-55ae-555a-a7e8-48475fa96d55" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542200 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi , & \\, & \\, & 2x-\\frac{\\pi }{6} & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & =2n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & 2x & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}+2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\frac{3n\\pi }{3} \\\\ x & =n\\pi  & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{(2+3n)\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\pi $ and $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=0,\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\pi ,\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{2}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ and ${x}_{3}=\\pi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\pi =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\frac{3\\pi }{3}=\\frac{5\\pi }{3}.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542201 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541993 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542201 1069 "q-121189" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542201 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542201 1071 "Consider the equation $\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-1=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4},{x}_{5},{x}_{6}$ are written in ascending order, what is the numerical value of ${x}_{2}+{x}_{4}?$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542201 1072 #uuid "bd3c6406-7b28-59e8-9519-0dcd8ba0850a" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542201 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nFirst, we rearrange the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x-1 & =0 \\\\ \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & ={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & 3x & ={135}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{45}^{∘}}{3}+\\frac{n\\cdot {360}^{∘}}{3} & \\, & \\, & x & =\\frac{{135}^{∘}}{3}+\\frac{n\\cdot {360}^{∘}}{3} \\\\ x & ={15}^{∘}+n\\cdot {120}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={45}^{∘}+n\\cdot {120}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={15}^{∘}$ and $x={45}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={135}^{∘}$ and $x={165}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x={255}^{∘}$ and $x={285}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4},{x}_{5},{x}_{6}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx={15}^{∘},{45}^{∘},{135}^{∘},{165}^{∘},{255}^{∘},{285}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${x}_{2}={45}^{∘}$ and ${x}_{4}={165}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}+{x}_{4}=45+165=210.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542202 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541968 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542202 1069 "q-103815" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542202 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542202 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent line to the polar curve $r={e}^{-\\theta }$ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta )={e}^{-\\theta }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-{e}^{-\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542202 1072 #uuid "be4431be-3fe9-5dd2-82ee-71868596c05f" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542202 1074 "To find the equation of the tangent line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta )={e}^{-\\theta }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-{e}^{-\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-\\theta }(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{{e}^{-\\theta }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\cancel{{e}^{-\\theta }}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}{\\cancel{{e}^{-\\theta }}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi /4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4} \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\theta }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & ={e}^{-\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4},\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent:\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4} & =0\\cdot (x-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}) \\\\ y-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4} & =0 \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{e}^{-\\pi /4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/6b/6b8602110483fcc09d9ac0c04a16ab4e624f00a2ff752851e9c027bef5c9bc83.png)" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542203 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542040 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542203 1069 "q-266026" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542203 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542203 1071 "![](images/6d/6d1f3a4ec50d76a52dc6a3a989d3779b529058d0de9c31d627d76a139884ff58.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the angle $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ in the coordinate plane. Expressed in radians, what is its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}?$\n\n${\\theta }_{R}\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542203 1072 #uuid "c65c1e13-f381-5212-802a-8ad773b76030" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542203 1074 "![](images/46/4672b0e5bf7db7d8eecc659ecd988c94d7e290c426a673d82d9b3299f02dc20b.png)\n\nThe reference angle, denoted ${\\theta }_{R},$  is the angle that $\\theta $ makes with the $x$-axis.\n\nAs $\\theta $ is in the fourth quadrant, its reference angle is given by\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}=2\\pi -\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}=2\\pi -\\frac{11\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542007 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542075 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542076 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542204 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542095 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542204 1069 "q-280018" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542204 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542204 1071 "Consider the equation $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-{4}^{∘})-1=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are written in **ascending order**, what are their values?\n\n${x}_{1}=${{field-1}}${}^{∘},\\,$\n${x}_{2}=${{field-2}}${}^{∘},\\,$\n${x}_{3}=${{field-3}}${}^{∘},\\,$\n${x}_{4}=${{field-4}}${}^{∘}$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542204 1072 #uuid "c6e2414a-60ee-52e9-b582-38de6779c295" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542204 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nFirst, we rearrange the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-{4}^{∘})-1 & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-{4}^{∘}) & =1 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x-{4}^{∘}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe general solution to our equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-{4}^{∘} & ={30}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}, & \\, & \\, & 2x-{4}^{∘} & ={150}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the two equations above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & ={34}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & 2x & ={154}^{∘}+n\\cdot {360}^{∘} \\\\ x & ={17}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘} & \\, & \\, & x & ={77}^{∘}+n\\cdot {180}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x={17}^{∘}$ and $x={77}^{∘}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x={197}^{∘}$ and $x={257}^{∘}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}.$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx={17}^{∘},{77}^{∘},{197}^{∘},{257}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}={17}^{∘},\\,{x}_{2}={77}^{∘},\\,{x}_{3}={197}^{∘},\\,{x}_{4}={257}^{∘}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542205 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542103 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542205 1069 "q-141419" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542205 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542205 1071 "Find the equation of the **normal** line to the polar curve $r=1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ at $\\theta =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542205 1072 #uuid "c7a90aa7-52b9-549e-8c2d-341971525e44" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542205 1074 "To find the equation of the normal line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =2\\pi /3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3})}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{2\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{-\\frac{1}{2}-2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})}{2\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2})}{(-\\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2})} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}))\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =(1-2(-\\frac{1}{2}))(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-1 \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}))\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =(1-2(-\\frac{1}{2}))\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(-1,\\sqrt{3}).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the normal:\n\nWe find the equation of the normal as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{m}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\sqrt{3} & =3\\sqrt{3}\\cdot (x-(-1)) \\\\ y-\\sqrt{3} & =3\\sqrt{3}x+3\\sqrt{3} \\\\ y & =3\\sqrt{3}x+4\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/ba/ba0b04158944bd15aeea3220d4aa63321dd39c423a4f6646ba59c299fa8ef736.png)" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542206 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542070 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542206 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542088 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542206 1069 "q-351547" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542206 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542206 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation of the normal line to the polar curve $r=4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ), \\\\ {x}^{′}(\\theta )=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the normal line is $y=$\n{{field-1}} $x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542206 1072 #uuid "c83da1a3-053f-5553-bc32-57bdb774a02e" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542206 1074 "To find the equation of the normal line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}(\\theta )=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\\,{x}^{′}(\\theta )=2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )+4\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )-4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})+4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})}{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})-4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{2+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{2-\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot {(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the normal:\n\nWe find the equation of the normal as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{m}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =\\frac{\\pi -2}{2}(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\ y & =\\frac{\\pi -2}{2}\\,x+\\frac{\\pi (4-\\pi )}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/2b/2bb2678804e79eb60cf9e1b16df60b8dad4ec4ccf8b2a9234a838c394ab9f3ac.png)" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542207 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542027 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542207 1069 "q-134005" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542207 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542207 1071 "![](images/af/af17a33ebaa130e3753ce1b13c32ae75cd208f6f76e5a4e710a838d736eaac27.png)\n\nWhich of the following is the trend line of the scatter plot above?" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542207 1072 #uuid "c870dc27-2799-528d-9132-ee7dc9d0d8d2" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542207 1074 "From the plot, it seems that the data points are scattered randomly. Therefore, there is no linear trend." 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542208 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542067 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542208 1069 "q-266024" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542208 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542208 1071 "![](images/44/44097b3c27d493dfb40698cdb743178a6720eb55c8019f63c4189108eb93dcf7.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the angle $\\theta =\\frac{10\\pi }{9}$ in the coordinate plane. Expressed in radians, what is its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}?$\n\n${\\theta }_{R}\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542208 1072 #uuid "c9ee09ae-09ff-5c56-b0b2-c24e54ab3c69" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542208 1074 "![](images/f2/f230c63f4a7768ba3e717e4ca69cea7d999fef83ae5fbaf1ebcd7af6697c1acb.png)\n\nThe reference angle, denoted ${\\theta }_{R},$  is the angle that $\\theta $ makes with the $x$-axis.\n\nAs $\\theta $ is in the third quadrant, its reference angle is given by\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}=\\theta -\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{10\\pi }{9}-\\pi =\\frac{\\pi }{9}.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542209 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541964 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542209 1069 "q-242913" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542209 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542209 1071 "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${4}^{3x-1}={9}^{x+6}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542209 1072 #uuid "ce76cbe9-5155-5439-bee7-2d4f7a68d153" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542209 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{3x-1} & ={9}^{x+6} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{3x-1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({9}^{x+6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n(3x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)=(x+6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) & =(x+6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ 3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ 3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ x(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542210 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542068 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542210 1069 "q-44032" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542210 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542210 1071 "Factor $-4{x}^{2}+4x-1.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542210 1072 #uuid "cf9a0034-0b04-5113-8b59-eaa614930923" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542210 1074 "Notice that the leading coefficient is negative. To reduce the expression to a more familiar form, we can factor out $-1,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{x}^{2}+4x-1 & = \\\\ -(4{x}^{2}-4x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor the quadratic expression inside the parentheses using the square of a difference formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-4x+1 & = \\\\ (2x)^{2}-4x+{1}^{2} & = \\\\ (2x)^{2}-2\\cdot (2x)+{1}^{2} & = \\\\ (2x-1)^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(4{x}^{2}-4x+1) & = \\\\ -(2x-1)^{2} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542211 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542023 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542211 1069 "q-143049" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542211 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542211 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=1-4{x}^{2}$ for $x\\le 0.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542211 1072 #uuid "d281164a-5064-5f5c-ac1f-851a58f31d90" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542211 1074 "To find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=1-4{x}^{2}\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx=1-4{y}^{2}\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =1-4{y}^{2} \\\\ x-1 & =-4{y}^{2} \\\\ \\frac{1-x}{4} & ={y}^{2} \\\\ -\\sqrt{\\frac{1-x}{4}} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the negative square root because if $x\\le 0$ is the domain of $f,$ then the range of the inverse is ${f}^{-1}\\le 0.$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=-\\sqrt{\\frac{1-x}{4}}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542212 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542096 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542212 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542097 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542212 1069 "q-327804" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542212 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542212 1071 "In **ascending order**, rounded to two decimal places in radians, the solutions to the equation $2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=10\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ are\n\n${x}_{1}≈$\n {{field-1}}$,\\,{x}_{2}≈$ \n{{field-2}}." 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542212 1072 #uuid "d68734a8-20a2-53fe-99b5-ad01be577281" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542212 1074 "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{10\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\ \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{10\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\ 2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =10 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{10}{2} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(5)≈1.373\\,40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places.\n\nTo find the other solution in the given interval, we add $\\pi $ to the principal value. Hence,\n\n$$\nx≈1.373\\,40+\\pi ≈4.514\\,99\n$$\n\nrounded to $5$ decimal places. \n\nSo, in ascending order, rounded to two decimal places, the solutions are \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}≈1.37,\\,{x}_{2}≈4.51.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542213 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542106 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542213 1069 "q-21475" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542213 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542213 1071 "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-15\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to the nearest integer?" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542213 1072 #uuid "d9ae048b-af0f-521f-b439-497b724959ce" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542213 1074 "We divide both sides of the equation by $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and use the identity $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-15\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{15\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-\\frac{15\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} & =0 \\\\ 4\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}-15 & =0 \\\\ 4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x-15 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we rearrange the equation and isolate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =15 \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡x & =\\frac{15}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{15}{4})=75.068\\,582{…}^{∘}≈{75.069}^{∘}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nSince ${75.069}^{∘}$ lies inside the given domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={75.069}^{∘}.$\n\nTo generate more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}={x}_{1}+{180}^{∘}=75.068\\,582{…}^{∘}+{180}^{∘}≈255.069\\,{…}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is $75.069\\cdot 255.069≈19\\,148$\nrounded to the nearest integer." 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542214 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542057 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542214 1069 "q-242901" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542214 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542214 1071 "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${e}^{2x-1}=3\\cdot {8}^{x}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542214 1072 #uuid "e0454be3-0e05-5ea2-b188-0b8e56e969a3" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542214 1074 "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x-1} & =3\\cdot {8}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2x-1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot {8}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n2x-1=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot {8}^{x}).\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power and product laws of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({8}^{x}) \\\\ 2x-1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)+1 \\\\ 2x-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+1 \\\\ x(2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+1}{2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542215 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541983 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542215 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542087 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542215 1069 "q-362883" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542215 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542215 1071 "Find the Cartesian equation $y=f(x)$ of the tangent line to the polar curve $r=7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡2\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the tangent line is $y=$\n{{field-1}}$x\\,-$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542215 1072 #uuid "e1dd610f-bcb9-5742-82dd-3d71c2887869" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542215 1074 "To find the equation of the tangent line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡2\\theta .\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /6} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =7\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{4} \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =7{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =7{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{4},\\frac{7}{4}).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent:\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\frac{7}{4} & =\\sqrt{3}(x-\\frac{7\\sqrt{3}}{4}) \\\\ y-\\frac{7}{4} & =\\sqrt{3}x-\\frac{21}{4} \\\\ y & =\\sqrt{3}\\,x-\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/4d/4d58fd3550e70915f3bc2a5e8ecd64cc4ef6c154a84ca536a5ca6ef6ddf49450.png)" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542216 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541972 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542216 1069 "q-6211" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542216 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542216 1071 "What is $(2.624\\,5)^{0}?$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542216 1072 #uuid "e7583ab2-cbcb-5d88-b90e-238c2a3f8662" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542216 1074 "Any number raised to the power of zero is equal to $1.$ So, \n\n$$\n(2.624\\,5)^{0}=1.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542217 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542035 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542217 1069 "q-156602" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542217 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542217 1071 "What is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{280}^{∘}$ expressed in terms of a reference angle?" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542217 1072 #uuid "e92a8145-93d8-5d18-8c79-868664e2dfd3" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542217 1074 "To express $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{280}^{∘}$ in terms of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\nFirst, let's draw the angle ${280}^{∘}$ in the coordinate plane (CAST diagram):\n\n![](images/70/70c2ef3ed7982e487a23eb8332a3740d65800a9b3bac73b8209b2c3cdb9fa210.png)\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta ={280}^{∘}$ is in the $4$th quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & ={360}^{∘}-\\theta  \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{280}^{∘} \\\\  & ={80}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{280}^{∘}$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{tan}⁡{80}^{∘}.$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{280}^{∘}$ must be *negative* because the tangent ratio is *always* negative in the $4$th quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡{280}^{∘}=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{80}^{∘}.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542218 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541966 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542218 1069 "q-143083" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542218 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542218 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)={x}^{2}+4x-4$ for $x\\ge -2.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542218 1072 #uuid "eaa3b305-adcb-5660-ac47-ef5d46e7e553" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542218 1074 "First, we complete the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & ={x}^{2}+4x-4 \\\\  & =({x}^{2}+2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2})-{2}^{2}-4 \\\\  & =(x+2)^{2}-4-4 \\\\  & =(x+2)^{2}-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTo find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y$:\n\n$$\ny=(x+2)^{2}-8\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y$:\n\n$$\nx=(y+2)^{2}-8\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(y+2)^{2}-8 \\\\ x+8 & =(y+2)^{2} \\\\ \\sqrt{x+8} & =y+2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+8}-2 & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we selected the positive square root because if $x\\ge -2$ is the domain of $f,$ then the range of the inverse is ${f}^{-1}\\ge -2.$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x)$:\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\sqrt{x+8}-2\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542219 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542039 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542219 1069 "q-124355" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542219 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542219 1071 "An angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane (measured in the usual way) has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{7}.$ Which of the following cannot be $\\theta ?$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542219 1072 #uuid "eb0cb59e-65d1-5e3c-b429-f78e92cffa51" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542219 1074 "Let's remind ourselves of the relationship between ${\\theta }_{R}$ and $\\theta .$\n\n![](images/35/35e9a04cf71b42acdf7769ef541c5b93c9267130e965d52d3989f3a00692cea1.png)\n\nUsing ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{7},$ let's calculate all possible values of $\\theta .$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the first quadrant, then $\\theta ={\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{7}.$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the second quadrant, then $\\theta =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{6\\pi }{7}.$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the third quadrant, then $\\theta =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{8\\pi }{7}.$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the fourth quadrant, then $\\theta =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R}=2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{13\\pi }{7}.$\n\nTherefore, $\\theta $ cannot be equal to $\\frac{5\\pi }{7}.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542220 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542101 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542220 1069 "q-334779" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542220 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542220 1071 "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=8y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-8x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following gives the general solution to the system of ODEs above?\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542220 1072 #uuid "ebc3a86d-4663-502d-a702-2a2557f07e8a" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542220 1074 "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 8 \\\\ -8 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nNow, using the given information, the standard fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ of the system is given by\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={Φ}_{0}(t)\\,c \\\\  & ={e}^{At}\\,c \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1} \\\\ {c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t)\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(8t)\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542221 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541965 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542221 1069 "q-20181" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542221 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542221 1071 "Find the slope of the tangent to the curve $y=\\sqrt{x-3}+3{x}^{3}$ at the point where $x=4.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542221 1072 #uuid "ed421aa6-2e1d-544f-a145-c01b981f6e83" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542221 1074 "To find the slope at a given point, we have to find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}$ at that point.\n\nLet $f(x)=\\sqrt{x-3}.$ We use the chain rule in the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=x-3.$ Then $f=\\sqrt{u}={u}^{1/2}.$ Differentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}u}=\\frac{1}{2}{u}^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the two together gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x-3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the sum rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x-3}}+9{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=4,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=4} & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{4-3}}+9(4)^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+144 \\\\  & =\\frac{289}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542222 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541986 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542222 1069 "q-102205" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542222 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542222 1071 "The inequality $0<x\\le 7$ can be expressed in interval notation as" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542222 1072 #uuid "eee859cf-032a-5a34-a81e-4f392e60f1ec" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542222 1074 "Every inequality can be expressed as an interval. We use the following notations:\n\n- Parentheses $()$ if the endpoints are *not* included.\n\n- Brackets $[]$ if the endpoints *are* included.\n\nFor the given inequality \n\n$$\n0\\,\\,<\\,\\,x\\,\\,\\le \\,\\,7\n$$\n\nwe note the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $0$ is *not* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $(\\,.$\n\n- The right endpoint $7$ *is* included in the interval. Therefore, we use $]\\,.$\n\nTherefore, the corresponding interval is\n\n$$\nx\\in (0,7].\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541970 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542006 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542019 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542223 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542022 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542223 1069 "q-279986" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542223 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542223 1071 "Consider the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡4x=1$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ If the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},{x}_{4}$ are written in **ascending order**, what are their values? Express your answers in terms of $\\pi .$\n\n${x}_{1}=${{field-1}}$,\\,$\n${x}_{2}=${{field-2}}$,\\,$\n${x}_{3}=${{field-3}}$,\\,$\n${x}_{4}=${{field-4}}" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542223 1072 #uuid "efa055ae-f23c-562f-bd3a-51db3280b097" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542223 1074 "We will find the general solution to the equation, and then generate all of the solutions in the required domain.\n\nThe general solution to our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the equation above for $x$ gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe set $n=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…,$ until we have generated all solutions in the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$\n\n- Setting $n=0$ gives $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$\n\n- Setting $n=1$ gives $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{8}$\n\n- Setting $n=2$ gives $x=\\frac{9\\pi }{8}$\n\n- Setting $n=3$ gives $x=\\frac{13\\pi }{8}$\n\nAll other values of $n$ generate solutions outside the domain $0\\le x<2\\pi .$ So, the solutions ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}$ (written in ascending order) are\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{5\\pi }{8},\\frac{9\\pi }{8},\\frac{13\\pi }{8}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that:\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\,{x}_{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{8},\\,{x}_{3}=\\frac{9\\pi }{8},\\,{x}_{4}=\\frac{13\\pi }{8}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542224 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542065 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542224 1069 "q-222063" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542224 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542224 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=-3{x}^{2}-1$ for $x\\le 0.$\n\n${f}^{-1}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542224 1072 #uuid "effbd8f4-b559-5948-841a-8a16bd6f2c1d" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542224 1074 "To find the inverse of a function, we follow these steps:\n\n**Step 1:** Replace $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\ny=-3{x}^{2}-1\n$$\n\n**Step 2:** Swap $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\nx=-3{y}^{2}-1\n$$\n\n**Step 3:** Solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-3{y}^{2}-1 \\\\ x+1 & =-3{y}^{2} \\\\ -\\frac{x+1}{3} & ={y}^{2} \\\\ -\\sqrt{-\\frac{x+1}{3}} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we take the negative square root because if $x\\le 0$ is the domain of $f,$ then the range of the inverse is ${f}^{-1}\\le 0.$\n\n**Step 4:** Replace $y$ with ${f}^{-1}(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=-\\sqrt{-\\frac{x+1}{3}}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542225 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542014 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542225 1069 "q-79002" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542225 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542225 1071 "Factor $16{x}^{2}-48x+36.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542225 1072 #uuid "f03b5992-4a97-5b3b-9545-c672d1a6e209" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542225 1074 "Notice that all of the terms have a common factor of $4.$ Therefore, we can simplify the expression by factoring out $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16{x}^{2}-48x+36 & = \\\\ 4(4{x}^{2}-12x+9)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's factor the expression inside parentheses using the square of a difference formula: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}-12x+9 & = \\\\ (2x)^{2}-12x+{3}^{2} & = \\\\ (2x)^{2}-2\\cdot (2x\\cdot 3)+{3}^{2} & = \\\\ (2x-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our original expression factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(4{x}^{2}-12x+9) & = \\\\ 4(2x-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542226 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541976 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542226 1069 "q-152384" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542226 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542226 1071 "Simplify $\\frac{\\sqrt{32{z}^{5}}}{\\sqrt{8{z}^{3}}}$ given that $z>0.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542226 1072 #uuid "f043c885-cf65-5aa1-b5b8-12a5b94bca34" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542226 1074 "First, we bring the quotient under a single radical and then simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\sqrt{32{z}^{5}}}{\\sqrt{8{z}^{3}}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{32{z}^{5}}{8{z}^{3}}}=\\sqrt{4{z}^{2}}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the expression inside the radical is a perfect square. So, we can continue simplifying by writing the expression as this perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{4{z}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{2}^{2}{z}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{{(2z)}^{2}} & = \\\\ |2z| & = \\\\ |2|\\cdot |z| & = \\\\ 2\\cdot |z| & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we are told that $z>0,$ so we have $|z|=z.$ Therefore, the final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot |z| & = \\\\ 2\\cdot z & = \\\\ 2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542227 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542100 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542227 1069 "q-138129" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542227 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542227 1071 "The maximum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=-3{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $5.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542227 1072 #uuid "f0a5dfca-c787-55e8-9cb2-9d81fe185f23" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542227 1074 "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542228 1068 #edb/ref 17592186542089 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542228 1069 "q-146227" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542228 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542228 1071 "| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ |\n\nSuppose $X$ and $Y$ are discrete random variables. Find $\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=2]$ given that $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ shown in the table above and the corresponding conditional expected value is ${\\mu }_{Y|2}=1.$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542228 1072 #uuid "f6596362-8154-5a9b-b6ee-af9895a85a60" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542228 1074 "Recall that the conditional variance of $Y$ given $X=x$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=x] & =\\text{E}[(Y-{\\mu }_{Y|x})^{2}\\,|\\,X=x] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}\\,|\\,X=x]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2} \\\\  & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)]-{\\mu }_{Y|x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order to find ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x),$ let's first find the marginal distribution for $X,$ corresponding to the row totals: \n\n| $f$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ | ${f}_{X}(x)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $\\frac{3}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{16}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ |\n\nThe conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x$ is\n\n$$\n{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)=\\frac{f(x,y)}{{f}_{X}(x)}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, dividing each joint probability by the corresponding row total, we obtain the conditional probability mass function of $Y$ given that $X=x:$\n\n| ${f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,x)$ | $Y=0$ | $Y=2$ | $Y=4$ |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{3}{8}$ | $\\frac{1}{8}$ | $1$ |\n| $X=2$ | $\\frac{3}{4}$ | $0$ | $\\frac{1}{4}$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y\\,|\\,X=2] & =[{\\sum }_{y}{y}^{2}\\,{f}_{Y|X}(y\\,|\\,2)]-{\\mu }_{Y|2}^{2} \\\\  & =[{0}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0+{4}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}]-{1}^{2} \\\\  & =0+0+4-1 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542229 1068 #edb/ref 17592186541989 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542229 1069 "q-149691" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542229 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542229 1071 "Find the equation of the tangent line to the polar curve $r=2\\theta $ at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ given that \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\n$$" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542229 1072 #uuid "f9519db7-867b-512b-ba13-465c2170cdc3" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542229 1074 "To find the equation of the tangent line to a polar curve at a point $P,$ we follow four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Calculate the derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}:$\n\nIn this case, we're given that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }.\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the slope of the tangent at $P:$\n\nThe slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{\\theta =\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0)}{(0-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 1)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P:$\n\nWe compute the $xy$-coordinates of $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =0 \\\\ {y}_{1} & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\pi ).$\n\n**Step 4**: Using point-slope form, find the equation of the tangent:\n\nWe find the equation of the tangent as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =m(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-\\pi  & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\cdot (x-0) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{\\pi }x+\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n![](images/13/13bb063a7ae79887a653e5f1c330851e56183049f77853a6a1d35d4a023d615c.png)" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542230 1058 #edb/ref 17592186542236 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542230 1062 #uuid "016be58b-34c7-56e0-bb25-ce68f3db4b2d" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542230 1063 #edb/ref 17592186542222 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542231 1058 #edb/ref 17592186542250 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542231 1062 #uuid "05c3145b-6657-521b-9f88-78380cc745b3" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542231 1063 #edb/ref 17592186542159 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542232 1058 #edb/ref 17592186542240 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542232 1062 #uuid "0a65f657-f98a-5a67-89c2-bc016942eb55" 13194139535918 17592186045417 true] [17592186542232 1063 #edb/ref 17592186542147 1